QB.add(9,[{"id":9001,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"Which statement about the circulatory system is correct?","o":["Cardiovascular and lymphatic systems working in parallel to move fluid","Cardiovascular and lymphatic systems working in series, with one pump each","Cardiovascular system only; the lymphatic system is part of immunity alone","Cardiovascular and respiratory systems working in parallel to move gases","Heart and arteries only; veins and lymphatics are separate systems"],"e":"The <b>circulatory system</b> = the <b>cardiovascular system</b> + the <b>lymphatic system</b>, working <b>in parallel</b> to transport fluid. Its main function is circulation: delivering <b>oxygen, nutrients and hormones</b> and <b>removing waste</b>.","w":["They work in parallel; only the CVS has a pump.","The lymphatic system is part of the circulatory system.","The circulatory system is CVS + lymphatics.","Veins are part of the CVS; lymphatics are included."],"k":"Circulatory = cardiovascular + lymphatic (in parallel).","rn":[["","What two systems make up the circulatory system?","What two systems make up the circulatory system?","The cardiovascular system; The lymphatic system; They work in parallel to transport fluid around the body"]]},{"id":9002,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"A student says “arteries carry oxygenated blood and veins carry deoxygenated blood”. Why is this definition wrong?","o":["Direction defines them: pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood away","Oxygen defines them: pulmonary veins carry deoxygenated blood to the heart","Direction defines them: systemic veins carry oxygenated blood to the heart","Wall thickness defines them: veins have thicker walls than arteries","Valves define them: arteries have valves and veins do not"],"e":"Arteries carry blood <b>away from</b> the heart; veins carry blood <b>towards</b> it. Oxygen content does not define them: the <b>pulmonary arteries</b> carry <b>deoxygenated</b> blood (to the lungs) and the <b>pulmonary veins</b> carry <b>oxygenated</b> blood (to the left atrium). Memory aid: Arteries go Away.","w":["Pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood; oxygen does not define them.","Systemic veins carry deoxygenated blood.","Arteries have thicker walls.","Veins have valves; arteries do not."],"k":"Arteries = away; veins = towards (not O₂ content).","rn":[["","Are arteries and veins defined by oxygen content or by direction of flow?","Are arteries and veins defined by oxygen content or by direction of flow?","Direction of flow; The pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood, but they are still arteries because they carry blood away from the heart; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":9003,"t":"Lymph Flow, Fluid and Fat Transport","s":"Which set of features describes the cardiovascular system?","o":["A closed network of vessels, with the heart as the pump","An open network of blind-ended vessels, with no pump","A closed network of vessels, with skeletal muscle as the pump","An open network of vessels, with the heart as the pump","A closed network that supplies only the lungs"],"e":"Key features: a <b>closed network</b> of blood vessels, <b>connecting all organs and tissues</b>, with the <b>heart as the pump</b>; arteries take blood from, and veins to, the heart.","w":["That describes lymphatic capillaries.","The heart is the pump.","It is closed.","It connects all organs."],"k":"Cardiovascular: closed, connects all, heart pumps.","rn":[["","Is the lymphatic system a closed system? Does it have a pump?","Is the lymphatic system a closed system? Does it have a pump?","Not a closed system; No pump"],["","How does the muscular (venous) pump work?","How does the muscular (venous) pump work?","Contracting muscles compress veins within a closed compartment (bounded by deep fascia); This pushes blood upwards towards the heart; Valves stop it falling back"]]},{"id":9004,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"What did William Harvey publish in 1628, and what had people believed before it?","o":["De Motu Cordis (circulation); before, arteries were thought to carry air","De Motu Cordis (circulation); before, veins were thought to carry air","De Motu Cordis (lymph); before, arteries were thought to carry air","The discovery of capillaries; before, arteries carried nutrient blood","De Motu Cordis (circulation); before, blood was thought not to move"],"e":"In <b>1628</b>, <b>William Harvey</b> published <i>De Motu Cordis</i> (“An Anatomical Exercise on the Motion of the Heart and Blood in Living Beings”), giving an account of the <b>circulation of blood</b>. Before this, from <b>Galen (2nd century AD)</b>, people assumed <b>arteries carried air or “spirit”</b> and <b>veins carried nutrient blood</b>.","w":["Galen: arteries carried air/spirit; veins nutrient blood.","It described blood circulation.","Not stated.","Not the earlier belief described."],"k":"Harvey 1628 vs Galen's “spirit” in arteries.","rn":[["","Where is blood distributed in the systemic circulation?","Where is blood distributed in the systemic circulation?","Mostly Veins; Then Arteries; Least in capillaries"],["","Are arteries and veins defined by oxygen content or by direction of flow?","Are arteries and veins defined by oxygen content or by direction of flow?","Direction of flow; The pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood, but they are still arteries because they carry blood away from the heart; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":9005,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"Why does blood flow from arteries to capillaries to veins?","o":["Blood flows from high to lower pressure, down the gradient","Blood flows from low to higher pressure, up the gradient","Veins actively suck blood from the capillaries","Capillaries have the highest pressure in the system","Gravity alone moves blood from arteries to veins"],"e":"Principle 1: like all fluids, blood flows from <b>high to lower pressure</b> – in the direction of the decreasing pressure gradient: <b>arteries → capillaries → veins</b>.","w":["Reversed.","Not stated.","Arteries have higher pressure.","Pressure gradient."],"k":"Blood flows down the pressure gradient.","rn":[["","Which direction does blood flow, and why?","Which direction does blood flow, and why?","From high pressure to lower pressure (\"like all fluids\"); In the direction of the decreasing pressure gradient; Arteries, then capillaries, then veins"]]},{"id":9006,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"Why is blood flow slowest in the capillaries, and why does this matter?","o":["Total cross-sectional area is greatest there, allowing exchange","Total cross-sectional area is smallest there, allowing exchange","Pressure is highest there, slowing flow to allow exchange","Walls are thickest there, slowing flow to allow exchange","Valves slow the flow there to allow exchange"],"e":"The velocity of flow varies <b>inversely</b> with cross-sectional area. As the <b>total</b> cross-sectional area increases, velocity decreases, so flow is <b>slowest in the capillaries</b>, giving time for <b>gas and nutrient exchange</b>.","w":["Greatest area, slowest flow.","Pressure is not the reason.","One cell thick.","No valves."],"k":"Largest total area → slowest flow → exchange.","rn":[["","How does the velocity of blood flow relate to the cross-sectional area of the vessels?","How does the velocity of blood flow relate to the cross-sectional area of the vessels?","Inversely; As the total cross-sectional area increases, velocity decreases"]]},{"id":9007,"t":"Arteries","s":"The thick layer between the internal and external elastic laminae is thicker in arteries than in veins. Name it.","e":"The tunica media contains smooth muscle and elastic fibres; it is thicker in arteries than veins because arteries carry blood at higher pressure.","k":"Tunica media.","img":{"src":"9/arterywall","cap":"Wall of a large artery, H&amp;E. Labels removed; layers numbered."},"a":["tunica media","media"],"rn":[["","Tunica media","Tunica media","The middle layer; Smooth muscle and elastic fibres; Thicker in arteries than in veins"]]},{"id":9008,"t":"Veins","s":"According to Poiseuille's law, if the radius of an arteriole halves while everything else stays the same, what happens to flow?","o":["It falls to 1/16","It falls to 1/2","It falls to 1/4","It falls to 1/8","It falls to 1/32"],"e":"Flow varies <b>directly</b> with pressure and the <b>fourth power of the radius</b>, and inversely with length and viscosity. (½)⁴ = 1/16, so flow falls to <b>1/16</b>. This is why arteriolar narrowing or widening is such a powerful control of flow.","w":["Radius to the 4th power.","That would be r².","That would be r³.","(½)⁴ = 1/16."],"k":"Flow ∝ r⁴: halve radius → 1/16 flow.","rn":[["","How do venous valves work?","How do venous valves work?","When blood flows upwards towards the heart, the cusps are pushed open; If blood tries to fall back, the cusps fill and close"]]},{"id":9009,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"Which statement of Ohm's law as applied to blood flow is correct?","o":["F = P / R","F = R / P","F = P × R","P = F / R","R = F × P"],"e":"<b>Ohm's law</b>: <b>F = P / R</b>, where F = flow, P = pressure gradient and R = vascular resistance.","w":["Inverted.","Not Ohm’s law.","P = F × R.","R = P / F."],"k":"F = P/R.","rn":[["","What is Ohm's law for blood flow?","What is Ohm's law for blood flow?","F = P / R; F = flow; P = pressure gradient; R = vascular resistance"]]},{"id":9010,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"Under Poiseuille's law, which change would increase flow through a vessel?","o":["Increasing the radius","Increasing the length","Increasing blood viscosity","Decreasing the pressure gradient","Decreasing the radius"],"e":"Flow varies <b>directly</b> with pressure and radius⁴ and <b>inversely</b> with length and viscosity. Increasing the <b>radius</b> increases flow (strongly).","w":["Decreases flow.","Decreases flow.","Decreases flow.","Decreases flow."],"k":"↑ radius or pressure → ↑ flow; ↑ length or viscosity → ↓ flow.","rn":[["","According to Poiseuille's law, what does flow through a narrow tube vary with?","According to Poiseuille's law, what does flow through a narrow tube vary with?","Directly with the pressure; Directly with the radius^4; Inversely with the length of the tube; Inversely with the viscosity"]]},{"id":9011,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"How is arterial pressure controlled?","o":["Independently: baroreceptor reflex short-term, renal–endocrine long-term","Dependent on cardiac output: baroreceptors short-term, renal long-term","Independently: renal–endocrine short-term, baroreceptor reflex long-term","Locally, according to tissue need, by arterioles and capillaries","By the force of contraction of the left ventricle alone"],"e":"Arterial pressure is controlled <b>independently</b> of local blood flow and cardiac output, mainly by the <b>baroreceptor reflex</b> (nervous, <b>short-term</b>) and the <b>renal–volume–endocrine system</b> (hormonal, <b>long-term</b>), to keep enough pressure for tissue perfusion.","w":["Independent of output.","Swapped.","That is local flow, not arterial pressure.","Not stated."],"k":"Arterial pressure: baroreceptors (short), renal–endocrine (long).","rn":[["","Baroreceptor reflex","Baroreceptor reflex","Nervous, short-term control of arterial pressure"],["","Renal–volume–endocrine system","Renal–volume–endocrine system","Hormonal, long-term regulation of arterial pressure"]]},{"id":9012,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"Which principle explains why an active, working muscle receives more blood?","o":["Flow to a tissue is controlled according to tissue need","Arterial pressure is controlled independently of flow","Flow is fastest where cross-sectional area is greatest","Little pressure remains after the capillaries","Blood flows down a pressure gradient"],"e":"Principle 2: the <b>rate of blood flow to a tissue is controlled according to tissue need</b>; an active tissue such as working muscle gets more blood.","w":["Different principle.","Slowest there.","Different principle.","True but not the answer."],"k":"Flow matches tissue need.","rn":[["","What controls the rate of blood flow to a tissue?","What controls the rate of blood flow to a tissue?","Tissue need; An active tissue (e.g. working muscle) gets more blood"],["","What are the 7 principles of circulation?","What are the 7 principles of circulation?","Blood flows down a pressure gradient (from high to low pressure); Flow matches tissue need; Arterial pressure is controlled separately from local blood flow and cardiac output; Velocity varies inversely with cross-sectional area; Resistance opposes flow (mostly due to vessel diameter); Little pressure is left after the capillaries; Venous return is not pushed by the heart; Physical laws apply (Ohm's law, Poiseuille's law)"]]},{"id":9013,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"Which mechanisms drive venous return, given that it is not the direct result of ventricular contraction?","o":["Skeletal muscle, breathing and venous smooth muscle","Ventricular contraction, breathing and gravity","Skeletal muscle, ventricular contraction and valves","Lymph node contraction and the baroreceptor reflex","Arterial pressure pushing through the capillaries"],"e":"Very little pressure remains after the capillaries, so venous flow is <b>not</b> driven by ventricular contraction. It depends on <b>skeletal muscle action</b>, <b>respiratory movements</b> and <b>constriction of smooth muscle</b> in venous walls.","w":["Not ventricular.","Not ventricular.","Not stated.","Little pressure remains."],"k":"Venous return: muscle pump, breathing, venous smooth muscle.","rn":[["","What drives venous return?","What drives venous return?","Skeletal muscle action; Respiratory movements; Constriction of smooth muscle in the venous walls"]]},{"id":9014,"t":"Veins","f":"ci11","s":"Which statement about the pulmonary and systemic circuits is correct?","o":["Pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood; pulmonary veins oxygenated","Pulmonary arteries carry oxygenated blood; pulmonary veins deoxygenated","Systemic arteries carry deoxygenated blood; systemic veins oxygenated","Pulmonary and systemic arteries both carry oxygenated blood","The pulmonary and systemic circuits are not connected"],"e":"<b>Pulmonary</b>: oxygen enters and CO₂ leaves the blood; <b>pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated</b> blood and <b>pulmonary veins carry oxygenated</b> blood. <b>Systemic</b>: arteries carry oxygenated blood to organs; veins return deoxygenated blood. The two are connected through the heart (a figure-of-eight).","w":["Reversed.","Reversed.","Pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated.","Figure-of-eight."],"k":"Pulmonary arteries deoxygenated; pulmonary veins oxygenated.","rn":[["","Pulmonary Vein","Pulmonary Vein","Vein that carries oxygenated blood; from the lungs; to the left atrium of the heart"],["","Are arteries and veins defined by oxygen content or by direction of flow?","Are arteries and veins defined by oxygen content or by direction of flow?","Direction of flow; The pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood, but they are still arteries because they carry blood away from the heart; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":9015,"t":"Veins","f":"ci6","s":"Trace one complete loop of the double circulation starting in the left heart.","o":["Left heart → body → right heart → lungs → left heart","Left heart → lungs → right heart → body → left heart","Right heart → body → left heart → lungs → right heart","Left heart → body → lungs → right heart → left heart","Left heart → veins → capillaries → arteries → right heart"],"e":"Left heart → <b>red arteries</b> → <b>body capillaries</b> → <b>blue veins</b> → right heart → <b>lungs</b> → back to the left heart: two circuits in a figure-of-eight with the heart in the middle.","w":["Left heart supplies the body.","Right heart supplies the lungs.","Right heart comes after the body.","Arteries first."],"k":"Double circulation loop.","rn":[["","Pulmonary Vein","Pulmonary Vein","Vein that carries oxygenated blood; from the lungs; to the left atrium of the heart"]]},{"id":9016,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","f":"ci12","s":"Where is most of the blood found at any moment, and why is this significant?","o":["Systemic veins (64–70%), acting as a reservoir","Systemic arteries (64–70%), acting as a reservoir","Systemic veins (10–15%), acting as a reservoir","Capillaries (64–70%), allowing exchange","Pulmonary vessels (64–70%), allowing exchange"],"e":"About <b>64–70%</b> of the ~5 L of blood is in the <b>systemic veins</b>, which act as a <b>reservoir</b>. Arteries hold 10–15%, capillaries 5–7%, pulmonary vessels 9–10%, heart 7%.","w":["Arteries hold 10–15%.","64–70%.","5–7%.","9–10%."],"k":"Veins hold 64–70% (reservoir).","rn":[["","Where is most of the blood found?","Where is most of the blood found?","Systemic veins (64–70%); Veins act as a blood reservoir"]]},{"id":9017,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"What is the correct order of vessels that blood passes through from the heart?","o":["Arteries → arterioles → capillaries → venules → veins","Arteries → venules → capillaries → arterioles → veins","Arterioles → arteries → capillaries → venules → veins","Arteries → arterioles → venules → capillaries → veins","Arteries → capillaries → arterioles → venules → veins"],"e":"Functional parts: <b>arteries → arterioles → capillaries → venules → veins</b>.","w":["Arterioles before capillaries.","Arteries first.","Capillaries before venules.","Arterioles before capillaries."],"k":"Artery → arteriole → capillary → venule → vein.","rn":[["","In what order does blood pass through the blood vessels?","In what order does blood pass through the blood vessels?","1. Arteries; 2. Arterioles; 3. Capillaries; 4. Venules; 5. Veins"]]},{"id":9018,"t":"Arteries","f":"ci13","s":"From outermost to innermost, which order of tunics is correct?","o":["Externa → media → intima","Intima → media → externa","Media → externa → intima","Externa → intima → media","Intima → externa → media"],"e":"Outside → inside: <b>tunica externa (adventitia)</b> → <b>tunica media</b> → <b>tunica intima</b> (memory aid E-M-I). The <b>external elastic lamina</b> separates externa from media; the <b>internal elastic lamina</b> separates media from intima.","w":["That is inside → outside.","Externa is outermost.","Media is middle.","Wrong."],"k":"E-M-I: externa, media, intima.","rn":[["","What are the three layers (tunics) of the vessel wall, from outside to inside?","What are the three layers (tunics) of the vessel wall, from outside to inside?","Tunica externa (adventitia); Tunica media; Tunica intima; MNEMONIC"],["","What separates the tunica externa from the tunica media?","What separates the tunica externa from the tunica media?","The external elastic lamina"],["","What separates the tunica media from the tunica intima?","What separates the tunica media from the tunica intima?","The internal elastic lamina"]]},{"id":9019,"t":"Arteries","s":"On this section of an artery wall, which structure is labelled <b>2</b>, the wavy dark line separating the innermost layer from the smooth muscle?","o":["Internal elastic lamina","External elastic lamina","Tunica adventitia","Vasa vasorum","Basement membrane of a capillary"],"e":"From inside out: tunica intima (endothelium), internal elastic lamina (wavy dark line), tunica media (smooth muscle and elastic fibres), external elastic lamina, tunica adventitia (externa) with vasa vasorum.","w":["The external elastic lamina lies at the outer edge of the media.","The adventitia is the outermost layer.","Vasa vasorum are small vessels in the adventitia.","Capillaries are not part of the artery wall layers here."],"k":"Wavy line under the intima = internal elastic lamina.","img":{"src":"9/arterywall","cap":"Wall of a large artery, H&amp;E. Labels removed; layers numbered."},"rn":[["","What separates the tunica media from the tunica intima?","What separates the tunica media from the tunica intima?","The internal elastic lamina"]]},{"id":9020,"t":"Arteries","s":"Name the outermost layer labelled <b>5</b>, which contains small vessels supplying the wall itself.","e":"The tunica adventitia (externa) is the outermost layer; it contains the vasa vasorum that supply the wall of large vessels.","k":"5 = tunica adventitia.","img":{"src":"9/arterywall","cap":"Wall of a large artery, H&amp;E. Labels removed; layers numbered."},"a":["tunica adventitia","adventitia","tunica externa","externa"],"rn":[["","Tunica externa (adventitia)","Tunica externa (adventitia)","The outermost layer; Strong outer covering of connective tissue, collagen and elastic fibres; Connects the vessel to the surrounding tissues; Well developed in large veins"],["","What are the three layers (tunics) of the vessel wall, from outside to inside?","What are the three layers (tunics) of the vessel wall, from outside to inside?","Tunica externa (adventitia); Tunica media; Tunica intima; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":9021,"t":"Arteries","s":"What is the endothelium?","o":["A single layer of squamous cells lining the tunica intima","A single layer of squamous cells forming the tunica externa","A layer of smooth muscle forming the tunica media","A layer of elastic fibres lining the tunica intima","A layer of squamous cells found only in veins"],"e":"The <b>endothelium</b> is a <b>single layer of squamous epithelial cells</b> in the tunica intima of blood and lymphatic vessels. Capillaries consist only of endothelium (plus basement membrane).","w":["Intima.","Media is muscle.","Elastic lamina.","All vessels."],"k":"Endothelium = single squamous layer.","rn":[["","Endothelium","Endothelium","A single layer of squamous epithelial cells; The internal lining in the tunica intima of blood and lymphatic vessels"]]},{"id":9022,"t":"Arteries","s":"What is the role of the elastic fibres in the adventitia and the external elastic lamina?","o":["Allow stretch, prevent over-expansion; allow diameter change","Prevent any stretch at all, keeping the vessel diameter fixed under pressure","Stop backflow of blood, acting as valves in the vessel wall","Allow exchange of gases across the vessel wall","Contract to push blood forwards like smooth muscle"],"e":"Elastic fibres in the adventitia let vessels <b>stretch</b> while <b>preventing over-expansion</b> from blood pressure; the <b>external elastic lamina</b> is a deformable, <b>compliant</b> structure allowing <b>diameter changes</b>.","w":["They allow stretch.","Valves are intimal projections.","Capillaries.","Smooth muscle does that."],"k":"Elastic layers: stretch without over-expansion."},{"id":9023,"t":"Arteries","s":"How does the tunica intima of veins differ from that of arteries?","o":["Veins: valves, no elastic membrane; arteries: elastic membrane","Veins: elastic membrane and valves; arteries: neither","Veins: elastic membrane, no valves; arteries: valves","Veins: a thicker intima with more elastic tissue than arteries have","Veins and arteries have identical intimal layers"],"e":"Arteries: intima = <b>elastic membrane lining + smooth endothelium</b>. Veins: <b>no elastic membrane</b>, but they contain <b>valves</b> (inward projections of the intima) to keep blood flowing one way.","w":["Veins lack the membrane.","Arteries have no valves.","Veins lack the elastic membrane.","Not identical."],"k":"Vein intima: no elastic membrane; valves.","rn":[["","Tunica Intima in Veins","Tunica Intima in Veins","no elastic membrane; contain valves to keep blood flowing one way"]]},{"id":9024,"t":"Arteries","s":"The aorta and pulmonary arteries stretch with each beat and recoil between beats. What type of artery are they?","e":"Large elastic (conducting) arteries contain more elastic tissue: they stretch with each beat and recoil.","k":"Elastic (conducting).","a":["elastic","elastic artery","elastic arteries","conducting","conducting arteries","large elastic arteries"],"rn":[["","Large elastic (conducting) arteries","Large elastic (conducting) arteries","Nearest the heart; More elastic tissue in the tunica media; Conduct blood away from the heart"],["","How does elastic artery act like a balloon?","How does elastic artery act like a balloon?","stretch when the heart squeezes, spring back between beats"],["","What are the 3 types of arteries?","What are the 3 types of arteries?","Large elastic (conducting) arteries; Medium / muscular (distributing) arteries; Small arteries and arterioles"]]},{"id":9025,"t":"Arteries","s":"Why are elastic arteries compared to a balloon?","o":["They stretch with each beat and recoil between beats","They stretch with each beat and stay stretched","They narrow and widen to direct blood to regions","They store blood as a reservoir between beats","They have valves that close between beats"],"e":"Elastic arteries <b>expand and recoil</b> during each cardiac cycle, helping maintain a <b>relatively constant pressure gradient</b> despite the heart's pulsing action – like a balloon stretching and springing back.","w":["They recoil.","That is muscular arteries (taps).","Veins.","No valves."],"k":"Elastic recoil smooths pressure.","rn":[["","How does elastic artery act like a balloon?","How does elastic artery act like a balloon?","stretch when the heart squeezes, spring back between beats"]]},{"id":9026,"t":"Arteries","s":"Which type of artery regulates its diameter to distribute blood to different regions?","e":"Medium (muscular, distributing) arteries regulate diameter to control regional flow.","k":"Muscular (distributing).","a":["muscular","muscular artery","muscular arteries","distributing","medium muscular arteries","distributing arteries"],"rn":[["","Medium / muscular (distributing) arteries","Medium / muscular (distributing) arteries","More smooth muscle in the tunica media than elastic arteries; Regulate their diameter to control blood flow to regions"]]},{"id":9027,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"What are the functions of small arteries and arterioles?","o":["Control capillary filling and contribute to arterial pressure","Control capillary filling and act as the main blood reservoir","Conduct blood from the heart and recoil between beats","Regulate flow to regions and act as the main reservoir","Allow exchange of gases and nutrients with tissues"],"e":"<b>Small arteries and arterioles</b> <b>control capillary filling</b> and <b>contribute to arterial pressure</b>. Their narrowing/widening powerfully controls flow (radius⁴).","w":["Veins are the reservoir.","Elastic arteries.","Veins are the reservoir.","Capillaries."],"k":"Arterioles: capillary filling + arterial pressure.","rn":[["","Baroreceptor reflex","Baroreceptor reflex","Nervous, short-term control of arterial pressure"],["","At the arterial end of a capillary, what happens to fluid?","At the arterial end of a capillary, what happens to fluid?","Net filtration (fluid out); Pushing pressure wins; About 20 L/day"],["","What are the 7 principles of circulation?","What are the 7 principles of circulation?","Blood flows down a pressure gradient (from high to low pressure); Flow matches tissue need; Arterial pressure is controlled separately from local blood flow and cardiac output; Velocity varies inversely with cross-sectional area; Resistance opposes flow (mostly due to vessel diameter); Little pressure is left after the capillaries; Venous return is not pushed by the heart; Physical laws apply (Ohm's law, Poiseuille's law)"]]},{"id":9028,"t":"Arteries","f":"ci16","s":"Which vessels arise directly from the arch of the aorta?","o":["Brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, left subclavian","Brachiocephalic trunk, right common carotid, left subclavian","Left and right brachiocephalic trunks, left subclavian","Right and left common carotids, right and left subclavians","Brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, right subclavian"],"e":"The arch gives the <b>brachiocephalic trunk</b> (→ right subclavian + right common carotid), the <b>left common carotid</b> and the <b>left subclavian</b>. Only the right side has a brachiocephalic trunk.","w":["Right common carotid comes from the trunk.","Only one trunk (right).","Not directly.","Right subclavian is from the trunk."],"k":"Arch: brachiocephalic, left CCA, left subclavian.","rn":[["","What are the 3 branches of the arch of the aorta?","What are the 3 branches of the arch of the aorta?","Brachiocephalic trunk; Left common carotid artery; Left subclavian artery"]]},{"id":9029,"t":"Arteries","s":"The coronary arteries supplying the heart arise from which part of the aorta?","o":["Ascending aorta","Arch of the aorta","Thoracic aorta","Abdominal aorta","Common iliac arteries"],"e":"The <b>ascending aorta</b> arises from the left ventricle; its branches, the <b>coronary arteries</b>, supply the <b>heart</b>.","w":["Gives head and arm branches.","Continues through the thorax.","Gut and gonads.","Pelvis and legs."],"k":"Coronaries ← ascending aorta.","rn":[["","What are the parts of the aorta, in order?","What are the parts of the aorta, in order?","Ascending aorta; Arch of aorta; Thoracic (descending) aorta; Abdominal aorta; Then it splits into the common iliac arteries"]]},{"id":9030,"t":"Arteries","s":"Which pairing of artery and region supplied is correct?","o":["Internal iliac – pelvic organs","External iliac – pelvic organs","Internal iliac – lower limbs","Subclavian – head and neck","Common carotid – upper limbs"],"e":"<b>Internal iliac</b> → <b>pelvic organs</b>; external iliac → lower limbs; subclavian → upper limbs; common carotid → head and neck; abdominal aorta → abdomen and gonads.","w":["Lower limbs.","Pelvic organs.","Upper limbs.","Head and neck."],"k":"Artery–region pairings.","rn":[["","What do the common iliac arteries split into?","What do the common iliac arteries split into?","External iliac arteries; Internal iliac arteries"],["","Which 2 veins join to form the common iliac vein?","Which 2 veins join to form the common iliac vein?","External iliac vein; Internal iliac vein"]]},{"id":9031,"t":"Arteries","s":"Which branches come from the abdominal aorta?","o":["Coeliac trunk, superior and inferior mesenteric arteries","Coeliac trunk, brachiocephalic trunk and inferior mesenteric","Superior and inferior mesenteric, and left subclavian","Coeliac trunk, coronary and superior mesenteric arteries","Coeliac trunk, common carotid and inferior mesenteric"],"e":"The <b>abdominal aorta</b> supplies the abdomen and gonads; branches include the <b>coeliac trunk</b>, <b>superior mesenteric</b> and <b>inferior mesenteric</b> arteries.","w":["Brachiocephalic is from the arch.","Subclavian is from the arch.","Coronary arteries arise from the ascending aorta.","Carotid is from the arch."],"k":"Abdominal aorta: coeliac, SMA, IMA.","rn":[["","What are the 3 branches of the arch of the aorta?","What are the 3 branches of the arch of the aorta?","Brachiocephalic trunk; Left common carotid artery; Left subclavian artery"],["","What are the 2 venae cavae?","What are the 2 venae cavae?","SVC (superior vena cava); IVC (inferior vena cava)"]]},{"id":9032,"t":"Arteries","s":"Which labelling of the vessels numbered 1, 2, 4 and 7 is correct?","o":["1 brachiocephalic trunk; 2 ascending aorta; 4 left common carotid; 7 descending aorta","1 ascending aorta; 2 brachiocephalic trunk; 4 left subclavian; 7 descending aorta","1 brachiocephalic trunk; 2 ascending aorta; 4 left subclavian; 7 arch of aorta","1 left common carotid; 2 ascending aorta; 4 brachiocephalic trunk; 7 descending aorta","1 brachiocephalic trunk; 2 right coronary artery; 4 left common carotid; 7 descending aorta"],"e":"From the arch of the aorta (6) arise, in order, the brachiocephalic trunk (1), the left common carotid (4) and the left subclavian (5). The ascending aorta (2) gives the coronary arteries (3 = right coronary); the arch continues as the descending (thoracic) aorta (7).","w":["2 leaves the heart as the ascending aorta; 1 is the first branch of the arch.","4 lies between the brachiocephalic trunk and the left subclavian: the left common carotid; 7 continues down as the descending aorta.","The left common carotid is the first branch on the left side of the arch.","2 is the ascending aorta; the right coronary artery (3) arises from the aortic root."],"k":"Arch branches: brachiocephalic, L common carotid, L subclavian.","img":{"src":"9/aorticarch","cap":"Aorta and its branches, anterior view. Labels removed; vessels numbered."},"rn":[["","What are the 3 branches of the arch of the aorta?","What are the 3 branches of the arch of the aorta?","Brachiocephalic trunk; Left common carotid artery; Left subclavian artery"]]},{"id":9033,"t":"Arteries","s":"What is an anastomosis and why is it important?","o":["A union of vessel branches that keeps tissue perfused if one is blocked","A single artery supplying tissue with no contribution from others","A union of veins and lymphatics at the root of the neck","A valve formed by inward projections of the intima","A capillary with gaps that lets cells through"],"e":"An <b>anastomosis</b> is a network formed by the <b>union of branches</b>, giving intercommunication. It <b>ensures continued tissue perfusion when one artery is compromised</b>.","w":["End artery.","Venous angle.","Venous valve.","Sinusoid."],"k":"Anastomosis = back-up route.","rn":[["","Anastomosis","Anastomosis","A network or connection formed by the union of branches (e.g. of blood vessels), giving intercommunication; Ensures continued tissue perfusion when one artery is compromised"]]},{"id":9034,"t":"Arteries","s":"Which pairing of region and anastomosis is correct?","o":["Brain – circle of Willis; hand – palmar arches","Brain – palmar arches; hand – circle of Willis","Brain – circle of Willis; hand – marginal artery","Colon – circle of Willis; knee – genicular","Retina – circle of Willis; hand – palmar arches"],"e":"Examples: <b>brain – circle of Willis</b>; <b>joints</b> – around shoulder, elbow, knee (genicular); <b>gut – marginal artery of the colon</b>; <b>hand – superficial and deep palmar arches</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Marginal artery is the colon.","Colon – marginal artery.","Retina is an end artery."],"k":"Anastomosis examples.","rn":[["","Give 4 examples of anastomoses","Give 4 examples of anastomoses","Circle of Willis; Joints: shoulder, elbow, knee; Marginal artery of colon; Palmar arches of hand"]]},{"id":9035,"t":"Arteries","s":"What is an end artery, and why is it clinically important?","o":["No significant collaterals; occlusion causes tissue death","Many collaterals; occlusion is usually compensated","No collaterals; occlusion causes only brief symptoms","The final artery before the capillaries in any tissue","An artery that supplies tissue alongside a vein"],"e":"An <b>end artery</b> supplies tissue <b>without contribution from other arteries</b>; it does not anastomose. <b>Occlusion</b> causes serious supply problems leading to <b>tissue death and loss of function</b>.","w":["That is an anastomosis.","Tissue death.","Not the definition.","Not stated."],"k":"End artery: no back-up → occlusion causes death.","rn":[["","Why are end arteries clinically important?","Why are end arteries clinically important?","Occlusion causes serious supply problems; Leading to tissue death and loss of function"]]},{"id":9036,"t":"Arteries","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> given as an example of an end artery?","o":["Circle of Willis","Central artery of the retina","Renal artery","Splenic artery","Coronary arteries"],"e":"End-artery examples: <b>splenic, renal, coronary, central artery of the retina, vasa recta</b> (and appendicular, perforating arteries in the notes). The <b>circle of Willis</b> is an <b>anastomosis</b>.","w":["End artery.","End artery.","End artery.","(Functional) end arteries."],"k":"Circle of Willis = anastomosis, not end artery.","rn":[["","Give 4 examples of anastomoses","Give 4 examples of anastomoses","Circle of Willis; Joints: shoulder, elbow, knee; Marginal artery of colon; Palmar arches of hand"]]},{"id":9037,"t":"Arteries","s":"How does an anatomical (true) end artery differ from a functional end artery?","o":["True: none; functional: anastomoses too small to help","True: small anastomoses; functional: no anastomoses","True: large anastomoses that help; functional: small anastomoses that do not","True and functional end arteries are identical","Functional end arteries have no blood flow"],"e":"<b>Anatomical (true)</b> end artery: <b>no</b> anastomoses. <b>Functional</b> end artery: anastomoses exist but are <b>ineffectual</b>. The coronary arteries are shown as functional end arteries.","w":["Swapped.","True end arteries have none.","They differ.","They do have flow."],"k":"True vs functional end arteries.","rn":[["","Functional end artery","Functional end artery","An end artery where anastomoses exist; but are ineffectual (too small to keep the tissue alive)"]]},{"id":9038,"t":"Arteries","f":"ci19","s":"A patient suddenly loses vision in one eye. Why can blockage of the central artery of the retina cause this?","o":["It is an end artery; each branch has no back-up route","It is an anastomosis; blockage cuts off all branches","It is a functional end artery with large anastomoses","It is part of the circle of Willis, which fails","It is a continuous capillary forming a barrier"],"e":"The <b>central artery of the retina</b> is an <b>end artery</b>: each branch supplies its own slice of retina with no back-up, so a blockage causes <b>sudden loss of vision</b> (the guide's figure explanation).","w":["End artery.","Functional ones have small anastomoses.","Not part of it.","Not a capillary."],"k":"Retinal artery occlusion → vision loss.","rn":[["","Why are end arteries clinically important?","Why are end arteries clinically important?","Occlusion causes serious supply problems; Leading to tissue death and loss of function"],["","What are the examples of end arteries?","What are the examples of end arteries?","Splenic; Renal; Coronary; Central artery of the retina; Vasa recta of the mesenteric arteries; Appendicular artery (from its midpoint distally); Perforating arteries"]]},{"id":9039,"t":"Veins","s":"Which set of features describes veins?","o":["Thin, elastic, large lumen, reservoir; some have valves","Thick, muscular, small lumen, reservoir; some have valves","Thin, elastic, small lumen, reservoir; none have valves","Thin, elastic, large lumen, high pressure; no valves","Thick, elastic, large lumen, reservoir; all have valves"],"e":"Veins <b>follow the course of arteries</b>, carry blood <b>towards</b> the heart, are <b>thin and elastic</b>, act as a <b>reservoir</b>, <b>lack large amounts of elastin and smooth muscle</b>, have a <b>large lumen</b>, and <b>some</b> have valves (not head and neck).","w":["Veins are thin.","Large lumen; some valves.","Low pressure.","Not all."],"k":"Vein features.","rn":[["","What are the key structural characteristics of veins?","What are the key structural characteristics of veins?","Thin, elastic walls; Lack large amounts of elastin and smooth muscle; Large lumen"]]},{"id":9040,"t":"Veins","s":"Which pairing of vein type and description is correct?","o":["Deep veins lie within muscle, near a corresponding artery","Superficial veins lie within muscle, near an artery","Deep veins lie near the skin, away from arteries","Pulmonary veins carry deoxygenated blood to the left atrium","Systemic veins carry oxygenated blood to the right atrium"],"e":"<b>Deep</b> veins: deep within muscle, near a corresponding artery. <b>Superficial</b>: near the skin, typically not near an artery. <b>Pulmonary</b>: oxygenated blood to the <b>left atrium</b>. <b>Systemic</b>: deoxygenated to the <b>right atrium</b>. <b>Venules</b>: smallest veins.","w":["Deep.","Superficial.","Oxygenated.","Deoxygenated."],"k":"Vein types.","rn":[["","Deep Vein","Deep Vein","Vein located deep within muscle tissue and typically near a corresponding artery"]]},{"id":9041,"t":"Veins","f":"ci22","s":"Which feature distinguishes large veins such as the superior vena cava?","o":["Wide longitudinal muscle bundles; well-developed adventitia","Wide circular smooth muscle and a thin adventitia","Valves in every segment and a thin adventitia","Fenestrations and an incomplete basement membrane","Thick elastic tunica media with a prominent internal elastic lamina"],"e":"<b>Large veins</b> (e.g. <b>superior vena cava</b>) have <b>wide bundles of longitudinal smooth muscle</b> and a <b>well-developed tunica adventitia</b>. Medium/small veins have valves; venules drain capillary beds.","w":["Longitudinal, well-developed.","Medium/small veins have valves.","Sinusoids.","That describes elastic arteries."],"k":"Large veins: longitudinal muscle, thick adventitia.","rn":[["","Large veins","Large veins","Wide bundles of longitudinal smooth muscle; Well-developed tunica adventitia; Example is the superior vena cava"]]},{"id":9042,"t":"Veins","s":"Which statement about venous valves is correct?","o":["Intimal projections; absent in the IVC, head and neck","Intimal projections; present in all veins including the head","Medial projections of smooth muscle; absent in the IVC","Adventitial folds; absent in the head and neck","Intimal projections; present only in the IVC"],"e":"Venous valves: an <b>inward projection of the intima</b>; <b>collagen and elastic fibres</b>; <b>semilunar cusps</b>; ensure flow <b>towards the heart</b>; <b>absent in the IVC, head and neck</b>.","w":["Absent in head/neck/IVC.","Intima.","Intima.","Absent in IVC."],"k":"Valve features and absent sites.","rn":[["","Where are unidirectional valves found (and not found) in veins?","Where are unidirectional valves found (and not found) in veins?","Present in most veins throughout the body; Absent in veins of the head and neck and IVC"]]},{"id":9043,"t":"Veins","f":"ci23","s":"How do varicose veins develop?","o":["Raised pressure dilates veins so valves cannot close","Raised pressure thickens valves so they cannot open","Low pressure collapses veins so valves stick shut","Arteries rupture into veins at high pressure","Lymph nodes compress the veins"],"e":"If veins <b>dilate</b> because of raised pressure, the valves may not close – <b>incompetent valves</b> – resulting in <b>varicose veins</b>, where blood pools and veins bulge.","w":["Valves fail to close.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Incompetent valves → varicose veins.","rn":[["","What causes varicose veins?","What causes varicose veins?","Veins dilate because of raised pressure; The valves may not close (incompetent valves); Resulting in varicose veins"]]},{"id":9044,"t":"Veins","f":"ci24","s":"How does the leg muscle pump return blood upwards?","o":["Muscles compress veins within deep fascia; valves stop backflow","Muscles compress arteries within deep fascia; valves stop backflow","Muscles relax and gravity draws blood up through valves","The heart pulls blood upward through the valves","Deep fascia contracts to push blood upward"],"e":"Return from the legs is <b>against gravity</b>. <b>Contracting muscles compress veins within a closed compartment</b> (deep fascia limits outward expansion), forcing blood <b>upwards</b>; <b>valves</b> stop backflow.","w":["Veins.","Against gravity.","Not ventricular.","Fascia limits expansion."],"k":"Muscle pump + deep fascia + valves.","rn":[["","How does the muscular (venous) pump work?","How does the muscular (venous) pump work?","Contracting muscles compress veins within a closed compartment (bounded by deep fascia); This pushes blood upwards towards the heart; Valves stop it falling back"]]},{"id":9045,"t":"Veins","s":"Why might someone feel lightheaded or collapse after standing still for a long time?","o":["Blood pools in leg veins, so less returns to the brain","Blood pools in the leg arteries, so arterial pressure rises too high","Lymph pools in the legs and compresses the leg veins","Venous return rises, overloading the heart","Capillaries in the brain close from low pressure"],"e":"The lecturer explained that without the <b>muscle pump</b>, blood <b>pools in the leg veins</b>, <b>venous return falls</b> and less blood reaches the brain.","w":["Veins pool blood.","Not stated.","Return falls.","Not stated."],"k":"Standing still → pooling → ↓ venous return.","rn":[["","Systemic Vein","Systemic Vein","Vein that returns deoxygenated blood from the rest of the body to the right atrium"],["","How do capillaries support the brain?","How do capillaries support the brain?","Form the blood-brain barrier; Deliver nutrients but keep toxins out"]]},{"id":9046,"t":"Veins","s":"Vessel 1 has a thick, round muscular wall and a small lumen; vessel 2 has a thin wall and a wide, flattened lumen. Which is correct?","o":["1 is an artery, because it carries blood at higher pressure","1 is a vein, because it carries a larger volume of blood","2 is an artery, because it stores blood between beats","1 is an artery, because arteries contain valves in the wall","2 is an artery, because arteries carry oxygenated blood"],"e":"Arteries have thick, muscular walls (thick tunica media) and small round lumens because they carry blood at higher pressure. Veins have thin walls, large often collapsed lumens and may have valves; they are a reservoir of blood.","w":["Veins are thin-walled with wide lumens.","Veins act as the blood reservoir.","Valves are a feature of some veins, not arteries.","Not all arteries carry oxygenated blood; vessel 2 is a vein."],"k":"Thick round wall = artery; thin collapsed = vein.","img":{"src":"9/arteryvein","cap":"Two blood vessels in cross-section, H&amp;E."},"rn":[["","Image card: Which is the artery and which is the vein?","Which is the artery and which is the vein?","1 Artery: thick, round, muscular wall and small lumen; 2 Vein: thin wall, wide, flattened / collapsed lumen; Arteries are thicker because they carry blood at higher pressure"]]},{"id":9047,"t":"Veins","s":"Is vessel <b>2</b> an artery or a vein?","e":"Vessel 2 is thin-walled with a wide, flattened lumen: a vein. Vessel 1, with its thick round muscular wall, is an artery.","k":"2 = vein.","img":{"src":"9/arteryvein","cap":"Two blood vessels in cross-section, H&amp;E."},"a":["vein","a vein","venule"],"rn":[["","Image card: Which is the artery and which is the vein?","Which is the artery and which is the vein?","1 Artery: thick, round, muscular wall and small lumen; 2 Vein: thin wall, wide, flattened / collapsed lumen; Arteries are thicker because they carry blood at higher pressure"]]},{"id":9048,"t":"Capillaries","s":"What are capillaries made of, and what do they connect?","o":["Endothelium on a basement membrane; arterioles to venules","Three tunics; arteries directly to veins","Endothelium on a basement membrane; arteries to veins","Smooth muscle and endothelium; arterioles to venules","Endothelium only; venules to lymphatics"],"e":"Capillaries are the <b>smallest, most delicate</b> vessels, made of <b>endothelium only</b> (plus basement membrane), connecting the <b>smallest arteries (arterioles)</b> to the <b>smallest veins (venules)</b>. Their main job is exchange.","w":["No tunics.","Arterioles to venules.","No media.","Not lymphatics."],"k":"Capillaries: endothelium only; arteriole → venule.","rn":[["","What are capillaries?","What are capillaries?","The smallest, most delicate blood vessels; Only have endothelium (plus basement membrane); Connect the smallest arteries (arterioles) to the smallest veins (venules)"]]},{"id":9049,"t":"Capillaries","s":"What is the approximate diameter of most capillaries, and what does this mean for red blood cells?","o":["8–10 µm; red cells pass in single file","8–10 µm; red cells pass side by side","8–10 mm; red cells pass in single file","1 µm; red cells cannot pass","80–100 µm; red cells pass in groups"],"e":"Most capillaries are about <b>8–10 µm</b> (1 µm = 0.001 mm), one cell thick, so <b>red blood cells pass in single file</b>.","w":["Single file.","Micrometres.","Too small.","Too big."],"k":"Capillaries 8–10 µm; RBCs single file.","rn":[["","Why do red blood cells pass through capillaries in single file?","Why do red blood cells pass through capillaries in single file?","Capillaries are so tiny (one cell thick, 8–10 µm)"]]},{"id":9050,"t":"Capillaries","s":"The kidney needs rapid exchange of fluid and small molecules. Name its capillary type.","e":"Fenestrated capillaries have pores (fenestrations) for rapid exchange: kidney, intestine, endocrine glands.","k":"Fenestrated.","a":["fenestrated","fenestrated capillary","fenestrated capillaries"],"rn":[["","Fenestrated capillaries","Fenestrated capillaries","Small openings (fenestrae) for rapid exchange; Found in the kidneys, small intestine and endocrine glands"]]},{"id":9051,"t":"Capillaries","s":"Name the capillary type with a complete lining that lets only small molecules through, found in the nervous system, fat and muscle.","e":"Continuous (least leaky) → fenestrated (pores, e.g. kidney, intestine, glands) → sinusoidal (gaps, incomplete basement membrane; liver, spleen, bone marrow).","k":"Continuous.","a":["continuous","continuous capillary","continuous capillaries"],"rn":[["","Where are continuous capillaries found?","Where are continuous capillaries found?","Nervous system; Fat; Muscle"],["","Put the capillary types in order from least to most \"leaky\"","Put the capillary types in order from least to most \"leaky\"","Continuous (sealed lining); Fenestrated (small pores); Sinusoidal (big gaps)"],["","What are the 3 types of capillaries?","What are the 3 types of capillaries?","Continuous (non-fenestrated); Fenestrated; Sinusoidal"]]},{"id":9052,"t":"Capillaries","s":"A liver biopsy shows capillaries with large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane. Name this capillary type.","e":"Sinusoidal capillaries (liver, spleen, bone marrow) allow even cells through.","k":"Sinusoidal.","a":["sinusoidal","sinusoid","sinusoids","sinusoidal capillary","discontinuous"],"rn":[["","Where are sinusoidal capillaries found?","Where are sinusoidal capillaries found?","Liver; Spleen; (Bone marrow)"]]},{"id":9053,"t":"Capillaries","f":"ci28","s":"Rank the three capillary types from least to most “leaky”.","o":["Continuous → fenestrated → sinusoidal","Sinusoidal → fenestrated → continuous","Fenestrated → continuous → sinusoidal","Continuous → sinusoidal → fenestrated","Fenestrated → sinusoidal → continuous"],"e":"From least to most leaky: <b>continuous</b> (sealed lining) → <b>fenestrated</b> (small pores) → <b>sinusoidal</b> (big gaps, incomplete BM).","w":["Reversed.","Continuous least.","Sinusoidal most.","Wrong."],"k":"Leakiness order.","rn":[["","Put the capillary types in order from least to most \"leaky\"","Put the capillary types in order from least to most \"leaky\"","Continuous (sealed lining); Fenestrated (small pores); Sinusoidal (big gaps)"],["","What are the 3 types of capillaries?","What are the 3 types of capillaries?","Continuous (non-fenestrated); Fenestrated; Sinusoidal"]]},{"id":9054,"t":"Capillaries","s":"Which pairing of organ and capillary function is correct?","o":["Bone marrow – lets new blood cells enter blood","Liver – forms the blood–brain barrier","Brain – removes defective red blood cells","Kidney – absorbs digested nutrients","Lungs – deliver hormones into blood"],"e":"Bone marrow: new blood cells enter blood. Brain: blood–brain barrier. Liver: removes defective RBCs and bacteria. Lungs: CO₂ out, O₂ in. Endocrine: hormones. Kidneys: peritubular capillaries filter, produce urine, absorb water and sodium. Small intestine: digested nutrients.","w":["Brain.","Liver.","Kidney filters, makes urine.","Endocrine glands."],"k":"Capillary functions by organ.","rn":[["","How do capillaries support the bone marrow?","How do capillaries support the bone marrow?","Let new blood cells enter the bloodstream"]]},{"id":9055,"t":"Capillaries","f":"ci29","s":"Along a capillary, what leaves the blood and what enters it?","o":["O₂ and nutrients leave; CO₂ and wastes enter","CO₂ and nutrients leave; O₂ and wastes enter","O₂ and wastes leave; CO₂ and nutrients enter","O₂ leaves; nothing enters the blood","Red cells leave; plasma enters"],"e":"Blood arrives oxygen-rich from the arteriole; <b>oxygen and nutrients</b> leave to body cells; <b>CO₂ and wastes</b> enter; blood leaves to the venule oxygen-poor.","w":["Wrong.","Wrong.","CO₂ enters.","Cells stay."],"k":"Capillary exchange directions."},{"id":9056,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> a function of the lymphatic system?","o":["Pumping blood around the body","Returning fluid to the bloodstream","Filtering lymph through nodes","Being part of the immune system","Transporting specific fats absorbed by the gut"],"e":"Lymphatic functions: complementary transport network; conveys and <b>filters lymph</b>; <b>manages fluid levels</b> by returning fluid to veins; part of the <b>immune system</b>; transports <b>fats</b> from the gut. It has <b>no pump</b>.","w":["A function.","A function.","A function.","A function."],"k":"Lymphatic functions; no pump.","rn":[["","What are the functions of the lymphatic system?","What are the functions of the lymphatic system?","Complementary to the blood vascular system; A transport network of delicate tubes throughout the body; Conveys lymph through a network of lymphatic channels; Filters lymph through lymph nodes; Helps manage fluid levels by returning fluid to the bloodstream (venous system); Part of the immune system; Transports specific fats absorbed by the gut"]]},{"id":9057,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"Which list contains only components of the lymphatic system?","o":["Lymphocytes, lymph, vessels, nodes, marrow, thymus, spleen","Lymphocytes, lymph, vessels, nodes, heart, thymus, spleen","Lymph, lymphatic vessels, nodes, liver, thymus, spleen and tonsils","Lymphocytes, lymph, vessels, nodes, kidneys, thymus","Red cells, lymph, vessels, nodes, marrow, thymus, spleen"],"e":"Components: <b>lymphatic cells</b> (lymphocytes), <b>lymph</b>, <b>vessels and plexuses</b>, <b>lymph nodes</b>, and <b>lymphatic organs</b>: red bone marrow, thymus, spleen, tonsils.","w":["Heart is CVS.","Liver is not listed.","Kidneys not listed.","Lymphocytes, not red cells."],"k":"Lymphatic components.","rn":[["","What are the lymphatic organs?","What are the lymphatic organs?","Red bone marrow; Thymus gland; Spleen; Tonsils"]]},{"id":9058,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"Which statement about lymph is correct?","o":["From capillary beds; like interstitial fluid; high protein; 2–3 L/day","From capillary beds; like plasma; low protein; 20 L/day","From the heart; like interstitial fluid; high protein; 2–3 L/day","From capillary beds; like interstitial fluid; no cells; 2–3 L/day","From capillary beds; like blood; high protein; 17 L/day"],"e":"Lymph originates in <b>tissue capillary beds</b>, is <b>almost identical to interstitial fluid</b>, contains <b>lymphocytes</b>, has a <b>relatively high protein</b> concentration, and normal flow is <b>2–3 L/day</b>.","w":["Not 20 L.","Capillary beds.","Contains lymphocytes.","Not blood."],"k":"Lymph features; 2–3 L/day.","rn":[["","What are the features of lymph (lymphatic fluid)?","What are the features of lymph (lymphatic fluid)?","Originates in tissue capillary beds; Almost identical to interstitial fluid; Contains lymphocytes; Relatively high protein concentration"]]},{"id":9059,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"Milky lymph containing absorbed fats, carried in the lacteals of the gut, is called what?","e":"Chyle: milky lymph with absorbed long-chain fats from the intestinal lacteals.","k":"Chyle.","a":["chyle"],"rn":[["","Chyle","Chyle","A special type of lymph from the gastrointestinal tract; Contains fats (cholesterol, glycerol, fatty acids) from intestinal absorption; Milk-like (opaque, milky) appearance; Carried in lacteals"]]},{"id":9060,"t":"Capillaries","f":"ci39","s":"Why does lymph flow equal about 2–3 L/day, in terms of capillary filtration and reabsorption?","o":["~20 L/day filtered, ~17 L/day reabsorbed; lymph collects ~3 L","~20 L/day reabsorbed, ~17 L/day filtered; lymph collects ~3 L","~3 L/day filtered; lymph collects all of it","~20 L/day filtered; lymph collects all of it","~17 L/day filtered, ~20 L/day reabsorbed; veins collect ~3 L"],"e":"At the <b>arterial end</b> pushing pressure wins: net filtration (~<b>20 L/day</b>). At the <b>venous end</b> osmotic pressure wins: reabsorption (~<b>17 L/day</b>). The leftover ~<b>3 L/day</b> is collected by lymphatics, matching normal lymph flow of 2–3 L/day.","w":["Swapped.","~20 L filtered.","~17 L reabsorbed.","Wrong."],"k":"Filtration − reabsorption ≈ lymph flow.","rn":[["","How do capillaries support the lymphatic system?","How do capillaries support the lymphatic system?","Collect fluid from tissues; Direct it to lymph nodes"],["","Where does lymph come from?","Where does lymph come from?","Fluid filters out of the blood capillary into the tissues (interstitial fluid); Most goes back into the blood; The leftover is picked up by the blind-ended lymphatic capillary and becomes lymph"]]},{"id":9061,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"Which set of features describes lymphatic vessels?","o":["Porous, blind-ended, small valves, thinner than veins, drain at the neck","Porous, blind-ended, no valves, thicker than veins, drain at the neck","Closed loops with valves, thinner than veins, drain into the heart","Porous, blind-ended, small valves, thinner than veins, drain into arteries","Thick-walled, valved vessels that drain into the right atrium"],"e":"Lymphatic vessels: <b>porous, blind-ended capillaries</b> that <b>converge</b> into larger vessels, have <b>small valves</b>, are <b>thinner than veins</b>, and drain into the <b>large veins at the root of the neck</b> at the <b>venous angle</b> (internal jugular + subclavian).","w":["Valved, thinner.","Blind-ended.","Veins.","Large veins at the neck."],"k":"Lymphatics: blind-ended, valved, drain at venous angle.","rn":[["","What are the features of lymphatic vessels?","What are the features of lymphatic vessels?","Porous, blind-ended capillaries; They converge into larger vessels; Drain into the large veins at the root of the neck, at the venous angle; Have small valves to prevent backflow; Thinner than veins"]]},{"id":9062,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"How do the overlapping endothelial cells at the blind ends of lymphatic capillaries work?","o":["They open when interstitial pressure exceeds capillary pressure","They open when capillary pressure exceeds interstitial pressure","They pump lymph actively into the vessel","They let fluid out but not in","They stay open, letting fluid flow both ways"],"e":"Lymphatic capillaries start <b>blindly</b>; the endothelium has <b>no basement membrane</b> at the blind ends and the cells <b>overlap</b>, shifting open when <b>interstitial pressure > intra-capillary pressure</b>. Fluid can <b>enter but not exit</b>.","w":["Reversed.","No pump.","In, not out.","One-way."],"k":"Overlapping cells = one-way entry.","rn":[["","Where does lymph come from?","Where does lymph come from?","Fluid filters out of the blood capillary into the tissues (interstitial fluid); Most goes back into the blood; The leftover is picked up by the blind-ended lymphatic capillary and becomes lymph"],["","What are the features of lymph (lymphatic fluid)?","What are the features of lymph (lymphatic fluid)?","Originates in tissue capillary beds; Almost identical to interstitial fluid; Contains lymphocytes; Relatively high protein concentration"],["","Which direction does blood flow, and why?","Which direction does blood flow, and why?","From high pressure to lower pressure (\"like all fluids\"); In the direction of the decreasing pressure gradient; Arteries, then capillaries, then veins"]]},{"id":9063,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"How do lymphatic capillaries compare in size with blood capillaries?","o":["Larger than blood capillaries, interspersed among them","Smaller than blood capillaries, interspersed among them","Larger than blood capillaries, fused with them","The same size as blood capillaries, separate from them","Smaller than blood capillaries, fused with them"],"e":"Lymphatic capillaries are <b>larger in diameter</b> than blood capillaries and are <b>interspersed</b> among them to collect interstitial fluid efficiently.","w":["Larger.","Interspersed.","Larger.","Wrong."],"k":"Lymphatic capillaries larger than blood capillaries.","rn":[["","What are the features of lymphatic vessels?","What are the features of lymphatic vessels?","Porous, blind-ended capillaries; They converge into larger vessels; Drain into the large veins at the root of the neck, at the venous angle; Have small valves to prevent backflow; Thinner than veins"],["","Where does lymph come from?","Where does lymph come from?","Fluid filters out of the blood capillary into the tissues (interstitial fluid); Most goes back into the blood; The leftover is picked up by the blind-ended lymphatic capillary and becomes lymph"],["","What % of blood is in the systemic capillaries?","What % of blood is in the systemic capillaries?","5–7%"]]},{"id":9064,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"Lymph from the lower limbs and abdomen collects in which structure, and by what route does it reach the blood?","o":["Cisterna chyli → thoracic duct → left venous angle","Cisterna chyli → right lymphatic duct → right venous angle","Thoracic duct → cisterna chyli → left venous angle","Cisterna chyli → thoracic duct → right venous angle","Cisterna chyli → thoracic duct → right atrium"],"e":"The <b>cisterna chyli</b>, a saccular dilation in the abdomen (most inferior part of the thoracic duct), receives lymph from the abdominal viscera, walls, pelvis, perineum and <b>lower limbs</b>; route: <b>cisterna chyli → thoracic duct → left subclavian vein</b> at the junction with the left internal jugular.","w":["Left side.","Cisterna first.","Left.","Venous angle."],"k":"Cisterna chyli → thoracic duct → left venous angle.","rn":[["","What is the route of lymph from the cisterna chyli?","What is the route of lymph from the cisterna chyli?","Cisterna chyli; Then the thoracic duct; Then the left subclavian vein (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins)"],["","Cisterna chyli","Cisterna chyli","A saccular dilation in the abdomen; The most inferior part of the thoracic duct"]]},{"id":9065,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"A tumour on the right side of the face spreads via lymphatics. Which duct does this lymph finally drain through?","e":"The right side of the head, neck, thorax and right arm drain via the right lymphatic duct.","k":"Right lymphatic duct.","a":["right lymphatic duct","right lymphatic"],"rn":[["","What does the right lymphatic duct drain?","What does the right lymphatic duct drain?","Most of the right upper quadrant of the body; Right upper trunk; Right upper limb; Right side of the head and neck"],["","Where does the right lymphatic duct empty?","Where does the right lymphatic duct empty?","At the junction of the right internal jugular and right subclavian veins"]]},{"id":9066,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"Lymph from the left arm returns to the blood via which duct?","e":"The thoracic duct drains everything except the right upper quadrant (right arm, right side of head, neck and thorax), which drains via the right lymphatic duct.","k":"Thoracic duct.","a":["thoracic duct","thoracic"],"rn":[["","What does the right lymphatic duct drain?","What does the right lymphatic duct drain?","Most of the right upper quadrant of the body; Right upper trunk; Right upper limb; Right side of the head and neck"],["","What does the thoracic duct drain?","What does the thoracic duct drain?","Everything else (roughly three-quarters of the body); Both legs, abdomen, left side above the diaphragm"]]},{"id":9067,"t":"Clinical: The Lymphatic System in Disease","s":"Why is knowing lymphatic drainage clinically important?","o":["To trace where a cancer came from or spreads","To work out where an artery has anastomoses with its neighbours","To estimate the patient’s blood pressure","To find where varicose veins will form","To locate end arteries at risk"],"e":"Knowing the <b>lymphatic drainage</b> of a region lets you work out <b>where a pathology (e.g. a cancer) has come from or will spread to</b>.","w":["That is arterial anatomy.","Not stated.","Venous.","Arterial."],"k":"Drainage maps track cancer spread.","rn":[["","What are the two types of cancer in lymph nodes?","What are the two types of cancer in lymph nodes?","Metastases (spread from elsewhere); Primary cancer (lymphoma)"]]},{"id":9068,"t":"Lymphatic Organs","s":"Which pairing of lymphoid organ type and example is correct?","o":["Primary – thymus, marrow; secondary – nodes, tonsils, spleen, MALT","Primary – spleen, tonsils; secondary – thymus, marrow, nodes","Primary – nodes, marrow; secondary – thymus, tonsils, spleen","Primary – thymus, spleen; secondary – marrow, nodes, MALT","Primary – thymus, marrow, spleen; secondary – nodes, MALT"],"e":"<b>Primary</b> lymphoid organs <b>produce lymphocytes</b>: <b>thymus, bone marrow</b>. <b>Secondary</b> lymphoid tissue is where immune cells function: <b>lymph nodes, tonsils, spleen, MALT</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Nodes are secondary.","Spleen is secondary.","Spleen is secondary."],"k":"Primary: thymus, marrow. Secondary: nodes, tonsils, spleen, MALT.","rn":[["","What are the secondary lymphoid tissues?","What are the secondary lymphoid tissues?","Lymph nodes; Tonsils; Spleen; MALT"],["","What are the lymphatic organs?","What are the lymphatic organs?","Red bone marrow; Thymus gland; Spleen; Tonsils"]]},{"id":9069,"t":"Lymphatic Organs","f":"ci38","s":"Which statement about the thymus is correct?","o":["Behind the sternum; fully formed at birth; involutes after puberty","Behind the sternum; grows through adulthood; primary organ","Left upper abdomen; fully formed at birth; involutes after puberty","Behind the sternum; forms after puberty; secondary organ","Behind the sternum; fully formed at birth; filters blood"],"e":"The <b>thymus</b> is in the <b>thorax, posterior to the sternum</b>, <b>fully formed at birth</b>, and undergoes <b>involution after puberty</b> (small and atrophied in adults). It is a <b>primary</b> lymphoid organ.","w":["Involutes.","Thorax.","Formed at birth; primary.","Spleen."],"k":"Thymus: retrosternal, involutes after puberty.","rn":[["","What happens to the thymus over life?","What happens to the thymus over life?","Fully formed at birth; Involution after puberty; Atrophied in adults"]]},{"id":9070,"t":"Lymphatic Organs","s":"Which set of functions belongs to the spleen?","o":["Filters blood, stores lymphocytes, removes old RBCs, recycles iron","Produces lymphocytes, involutes after puberty and recycles iron from haem","Filters lymph, stores lymphocytes, removes old red cells","Filters blood, produces red cells, carries chyle","Filters blood, forms the BBB, recycles iron"],"e":"The <b>spleen</b> – the <b>largest lymphatic organ</b>, fist-sized, in the <b>left upper abdomen</b> – <b>filters blood</b>, <b>stores lymphocytes</b>, reacts to <b>blood-borne antigens</b>, <b>removes old/defective red cells</b> and <b>recycles iron</b> from haemoglobin.","w":["That is mostly the thymus.","Blood.","Not stated.","Brain."],"k":"Spleen functions.","rn":[["","What are the 5 jobs of the spleen?","What are the 5 jobs of the spleen?","Filter blood; Store lymphocytes; React to blood borne antigens; Remove old erythrocytes; Recycle iron from haemoglobin"]]},{"id":9071,"t":"Lymphatic Organs","s":"Which statement contrasts red and yellow bone marrow correctly?","o":["Red: active haemopoiesis with sinusoids; yellow: inactive, adipose","Red: inactive, adipose; yellow: active haemopoiesis with sinusoids","Red: active haemopoiesis, mostly adipose; yellow: inactive","Red and yellow: both active in haemopoiesis","Red: only in adults; yellow: only in children"],"e":"<b>Red marrow</b>: active haemopoietic cells (in children fills the medullary cavity; in adults in spongy bone), with <b>sinusoids</b> where new cells are released. <b>Yellow marrow</b>: <b>not</b> active, mostly <b>adipose</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Yellow is adipose.","Yellow is inactive.","Red in spongy bone in adults."],"k":"Red marrow active; yellow marrow fatty."},{"id":9072,"t":"Lymph Flow, Fluid and Fat Transport","s":"How is lymph moved, given the lymphatic system has no pump?","o":["Smooth muscle, skeletal muscle and breathing, plus valves","The heart, skeletal muscle and breathing, with valves","Vascular smooth muscle and gravity, without valves","Spleen contraction and the thymus, with valves","Arterial pressure carried on through the capillaries into the lymphatics"],"e":"The lymphatic system is <b>not closed</b> and has <b>no pump</b>; flow is driven by <b>vascular smooth muscle</b>, <b>skeletal muscle contraction</b> and <b>respiratory movements</b>, with <b>valves</b> in larger vessels preventing backflow.","w":["No pump.","Valves.","Not stated.","Little pressure remains."],"k":"Lymph moved by muscles and breathing.","rn":[["","What drives lymph flow?","What drives lymph flow?","A pressure gradient created by:; Vascular smooth muscle; Skeletal muscle contraction; Respiratory movements"],["","What drives venous return?","What drives venous return?","Skeletal muscle action; Respiratory movements; Constriction of smooth muscle in the venous walls"]]},{"id":9073,"t":"Lymph Flow, Fluid and Fat Transport","s":"Which statement best contrasts blood and lymph circulation?","o":["Blood: closed loop with a pump; lymph: one way, no pump","Blood: one way, no pump; lymph: closed loop with a pump","Blood and lymph: both closed loops with pumps","Blood: closed loop, no pump; lymph: one way with a pump","Blood and lymph: both one way, from tissues to veins"],"e":"Blood: <b>closed loop</b> with the heart as pump. Lymph: <b>one way</b> (tissues → veins), <b>no pump</b>, pushed by muscles, breathing and valves.","w":["Swapped.","Lymph has no pump.","Heart pumps.","Blood is a loop."],"k":"Blood closed/pumped; lymph open/one-way.","rn":[["","Is the lymphatic system a closed system? Does it have a pump?","Is the lymphatic system a closed system? Does it have a pump?","Not a closed system; No pump"],["","How does the muscular (venous) pump work?","How does the muscular (venous) pump work?","Contracting muscles compress veins within a closed compartment (bounded by deep fascia); This pushes blood upwards towards the heart; Valves stop it falling back"]]},{"id":9074,"t":"Lymph Flow, Fluid and Fat Transport","f":"ci40","s":"How are long-chain fatty acids from the small intestine transported?","o":["As chylomicrons entering lacteals, forming chyle","As chylomicrons entering blood capillaries in villi","As free fatty acids in the portal vein","As chylomicrons entering sinusoids in the liver","As free fatty acids in lacteals, forming chyle"],"e":"In the small intestine <b>long-chain fatty acids</b> are coated with protein and packaged with cholesterol into <b>chylomicrons</b>, which enter the <b>lacteals</b> (lymphatic capillaries) – not the blood capillaries – forming milky <b>chyle</b>.","w":["Lacteals.","Chylomicrons.","Lacteals.","Packaged as chylomicrons."],"k":"Fats → chylomicrons → lacteals → chyle.","rn":[["","Do absorbed fats enter the blood capillaries or the lacteals?","Do absorbed fats enter the blood capillaries or the lacteals?","The lacteals (as chylomicrons)"]]},{"id":9075,"t":"Lymph Nodes and Lymphatic Drainage","s":"Which statement about lymph nodes is correct?","o":["All lymph passes through nodes; afferents in, efferents out","All lymph passes through nodes; efferents in, afferents out","Some lymph bypasses nodes; afferents in, efferents out","All lymph passes through nodes, which pump it on","Some lymph bypasses nodes; nodes produce red cells"],"e":"Lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped, along lymphatic vessels; <b>all lymph passes through</b> them. <b>Afferent</b> vessels take lymph <b>to</b> the node; <b>efferent</b> vessels take it <b>away</b> (Afferent = Arrives; Efferent = Exits). They filter, trap and phagocytose, and defend against antigens.","w":["Swapped.","All passes.","No pump.","Wrong."],"k":"Afferent in, efferent out; all lymph filtered.","rn":[["","What are the features of lymph nodes?","What are the features of lymph nodes?","Small, bean-shaped tissues along lymphatic vessels; All lymph passes through lymph nodes; Afferent vessels take lymph to the node; efferent vessels take lymph away; Elaborate filters that trap and phagocytose particulate matter; Detect and defend against foreign antigens"]]},{"id":9076,"t":"Lymph Nodes and Lymphatic Drainage","s":"Name the hypothetical first lymph node that drains a cancer.","e":"The sentinel node is the first node draining a tumour, important in staging and spread.","k":"Sentinel node.","a":["sentinel node","sentinel lymph node","sentinel"],"rn":[["","Sentinel node","Sentinel node","The hypothetical first lymph node draining a cancer"]]},{"id":9077,"t":"Lymph Nodes and Lymphatic Drainage","s":"Which statement about superficial lymphatics is correct?","o":["More numerous than veins; follow venous drainage; drain into deep vessels","Fewer than veins; follow arterial supply; drain into deep vessels","More numerous than veins; follow arteries; drain directly into veins","More numerous than veins; follow venous drainage; drain into the heart","Absent from the subcutaneous tissue"],"e":"<b>Superficial lymphatic vessels</b> are <b>more numerous than veins</b> in subcutaneous tissue, <b>follow venous drainage</b>, and drain into <b>deep lymphatic vessels</b>. <b>Superficial nodes</b> lie <b>superficial to the deep fascia</b>, with superficial veins, draining to deep nodes.","w":["More numerous.","Venous.","Deep vessels.","Present."],"k":"Superficial lymphatics follow veins.","rn":[["","Superficial lymphatic vessels: where are they and where do they drain?","Superficial lymphatic vessels: where are they and where do they drain?","More numerous than veins in subcutaneous tissue; Follow venous drainage; Drain into deep lymphatic vessels"]]},{"id":9078,"t":"Lymph Nodes and Lymphatic Drainage","s":"Which statement about deep lymphatics is correct?","o":["Accompany arteries; drain organs; nodes named after nearby structures","Accompany superficial veins; drain skin; nodes named by number","Accompany arteries; drain skin; nodes lie in subcutaneous tissue","Accompany arteries; drain organs; nodes named by number","Accompany superficial veins; drain organs; nodes named after nearby structures"],"e":"<b>Deep lymphatic vessels accompany arteries</b> and drain <b>internal organs</b>. <b>Deep nodes</b> lie close to vasculature and viscera and are <b>named after neighbouring structures</b> – pre-aortic, para-aortic, common iliac, internal and external iliac.","w":["Superficial.","Organs.","Named after structures.","Arteries."],"k":"Deep lymphatics follow arteries.","rn":[["","Deep lymphatic vessels: what do they follow and drain?","Deep lymphatic vessels: what do they follow and drain?","Accompany arteries; Drain internal organs"],["","How are deep lymph nodes named?","How are deep lymph nodes named?","After neighbouring structures (near vessels and viscera)"]]},{"id":9079,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"Put the flow of lymph in order.","o":["Interstitial fluid → capillaries → superficial → deep → trunks → ducts → venous angle","Interstitial fluid → capillaries → deep → superficial → trunks → ducts → venous angle","Interstitial fluid → capillaries → superficial → deep → ducts → trunks → venous angle","Venous angle → ducts → trunks → deep → superficial → capillaries → interstitial fluid","Interstitial fluid → capillaries → trunks → superficial → deep → ducts → venous angle"],"e":"Flow: <b>interstitial fluid → lymphatic capillaries → superficial vessels (following veins) → superficial nodes → deep vessels (following arteries) → deep nodes → lymphatic trunks → thoracic/right lymphatic duct → venous angle → blood</b>.","w":["Superficial first.","Trunks before ducts.","Reversed.","Trunks later."],"k":"Lymph flow sequence.","rn":[["","Where does lymph come from?","Where does lymph come from?","Fluid filters out of the blood capillary into the tissues (interstitial fluid); Most goes back into the blood; The leftover is picked up by the blind-ended lymphatic capillary and becomes lymph"],["","Superficial lymphatic vessels: where are they and where do they drain?","Superficial lymphatic vessels: where are they and where do they drain?","More numerous than veins in subcutaneous tissue; Follow venous drainage; Drain into deep lymphatic vessels"],["","What are the features of lymph (lymphatic fluid)?","What are the features of lymph (lymphatic fluid)?","Originates in tissue capillary beds; Almost identical to interstitial fluid; Contains lymphocytes; Relatively high protein concentration"]]},{"id":9080,"t":"Clinical: The Lymphatic System in Disease","s":"Which statement about lymph nodes in disease is correct?","o":["Metastases spread via lymphatics (often carcinomas); lymphoma is primary","Metastases spread via lymphatics (rarely carcinomas); lymphoma is secondary","Lymphoma spreads via lymphatics from carcinomas elsewhere","Metastases are always nearby; lymphoma is a red cell cancer","Metastases and lymphoma are both primary cancers of nodes"],"e":"<b>Metastases</b>: cancer spread from elsewhere <b>through the lymphatics</b>; <b>carcinomas</b> most common; spread can be <b>nearby or distant</b>. <b>Primary cancer</b>: <b>lymphoma</b> (Hodgkin or non-Hodgkin).","w":["Carcinomas most common.","Lymphoma is primary.","Can be distant.","Metastases are secondary."],"k":"Metastases vs lymphoma.","rn":[["","What are the two types of cancer in lymph nodes?","What are the two types of cancer in lymph nodes?","Metastases (spread from elsewhere); Primary cancer (lymphoma)"]]},{"id":9081,"t":"Clinical: The Lymphatic System in Disease","s":"A child develops swollen lymph nodes after a cat scratch. Which term and cause fit?","o":["Lymphadenopathy from a skin wound (Bartonella)","Lymphadenitis from an inherited lymphatic malformation","Lymphoedema from skin wound infection","Lymphangitis from Bartonella henselae","Lymphadenopathy from lymphoma"],"e":"<b>Lymphadenopathy</b> = swelling of lymph nodes. Causes include infection (bacterial, viral, fungal, filariasis), <b>skin wounds such as a cat scratch (<i>Bartonella henselae</i>)</b>, metastases and lymphoma.","w":["Not inherited.","Lymphoedema is swelling of tissue.","Nodes are swollen.","Infection, not lymphoma."],"k":"Cat scratch → lymphadenopathy.","rn":[["","What are the causes of lymphadenopathy?","What are the causes of lymphadenopathy?","Infection: bacterial, viral, fungal, or filariasis (threadworm); Skin wounds, e.g. cat scratch (Bartonella henselae); Metastases; Primary malignancy (lymphoma)"]]},{"id":9082,"t":"Clinical: The Lymphatic System in Disease","s":"What distinguishes lymphadenitis from lymphangitis?","o":["Lymphadenitis: inflamed nodes; lymphangitis: infected vessels (urgent)","Lymphadenitis: infected vessels; lymphangitis: inflamed nodes (urgent)","Lymphadenitis: swollen nodes from any cause; lymphangitis: tissue swelling","Lymphadenitis: lymphoma of nodes; lymphangitis: vessel metastases","They are two names for the same condition"],"e":"<b>Lymphadenitis</b>: inflammation of <b>nodes</b>, usually <b>bacterial</b>, <b>tender</b>. <b>Lymphangitis</b>: infection of lymph <b>vessels</b>; <b>ascending lymphangitis needs urgent medical treatment</b>. (-aden- = gland/node; -angi- = vessel.)","w":["Swapped.","That is lymphadenopathy/lymphoedema.","Infection.","Different."],"k":"-aden- node; -angi- vessel.","rn":[["","Lymphangitis","Lymphangitis","Infection of lymph vessels; Ascending lymphangitis needs urgent medical treatment"]]},{"id":9083,"t":"Clinical: The Lymphatic System in Disease","s":"A woman develops arm swelling after axillary lymph nodes are removed during breast cancer surgery. Name the condition.","e":"Lymphoedema caused by damage or removal of lymphatics (e.g. surgery) is secondary lymphoedema; congenital absence is primary.","k":"Secondary lymphoedema.","a":["secondary lymphoedema","lymphoedema","secondary lymphedema","lymphedema"],"rn":[["","Primary vs secondary lymphoedema","Primary vs secondary lymphoedema","Primary: inherited malformed lymphatics; Secondary: acquired dysfunction"],["","What are the causes of secondary lymphoedema?","What are the causes of secondary lymphoedema?","Trauma (including accidental surgical injury); Deliberate surgical removal (e.g. nodes removed in cancer surgery); Infection; Radiation therapy; Obstruction"]]},{"id":9084,"t":"Clinical: The Lymphatic System in Disease","s":"A baby is born without lymphatic tissue in one leg, which swells. Name the condition.","e":"Primary lymphoedema is due to congenital absence/malformation of lymphatics; secondary results from damage, e.g. surgery.","k":"Primary lymphoedema.","a":["primary lymphoedema","primary lymphedema","congenital lymphoedema"],"rn":[["","Primary vs secondary lymphoedema","Primary vs secondary lymphoedema","Primary: inherited malformed lymphatics; Secondary: acquired dysfunction"]]},{"id":9085,"t":"Arteries","s":"A blood clot blocks one branch feeding the circle of Willis, but the brain tissue survives. What explains this?","o":["The circle of Willis is an anastomosis giving another route","The circle of Willis is an end artery giving no route","The brain is supplied by veins when arteries block","The brain capillaries enlarge to compensate","The lymphatics supply oxygen to the brain"],"e":"An <b>anastomosis</b> ensures <b>continued perfusion</b> when one artery is compromised; the <b>circle of Willis</b> is the brain example.","w":["Anastomosis.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Anastomosis provides collateral flow.","rn":[["","Anastomosis","Anastomosis","A network or connection formed by the union of branches (e.g. of blood vessels), giving intercommunication; Ensures continued tissue perfusion when one artery is compromised"]]},{"id":9086,"t":"Arteries","s":"A coronary artery is suddenly blocked. Why does this cause a heart attack rather than being compensated?","o":["Functional end arteries: anastomoses too small","True end arteries: they have no anastomoses at all","They have large anastomoses that clot together","They are veins and cannot redirect flow","They have sinusoidal capillaries that leak"],"e":"The coronary arteries are <b>(functional) end arteries</b>: anastomoses exist but are <b>ineffectual</b>, so occlusion causes <b>tissue death</b> – a heart attack.","w":["They are functional end arteries.","Small.","Arteries.","Not stated."],"k":"Coronary = functional end arteries.","rn":[["","Functional end artery","Functional end artery","An end artery where anastomoses exist; but are ineffectual (too small to keep the tissue alive)"]]},{"id":9087,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"Which of the following would most increase resistance in a blood vessel?","o":["Narrowing its diameter","Widening its diameter","Shortening the vessel","Lowering blood viscosity","Increasing the pressure gradient"],"e":"Resistance is mainly due to <b>diameter</b>: as it decreases, resistance increases (Poiseuille: radius⁴). Longer vessels and higher viscosity also increase resistance.","w":["Decreases resistance.","Decreases resistance.","Decreases resistance.","Does not increase resistance."],"k":"Narrowing ↑ resistance.","rn":[["","What happens to resistance and blood flow as vessel diameter decreases?","What happens to resistance and blood flow as vessel diameter decreases?","Resistance increases; Blood flow decreases"]]},{"id":9088,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"Which statement about blood volume and veins is most clinically relevant to fainting on prolonged standing?","o":["Veins hold most blood and need the muscle pump, so pooling cuts return","Arteries hold most blood and need the muscle pump, so pooling cuts return","Veins hold most blood but are pushed by the heart, so return is maintained","Capillaries hold most blood, so pooling in them cuts return","Lymph holds most fluid, so pooling in it cuts return"],"e":"Veins hold <b>64–70%</b> of blood (reservoir) and venous return depends on the <b>muscle pump</b>; standing still lets blood pool in leg veins, reducing return to the heart and brain.","w":["Veins.","Not pushed by the heart.","Veins.","Not stated."],"k":"Venous reservoir + muscle pump.","rn":[["","Where is most of the blood found?","Where is most of the blood found?","Systemic veins (64–70%); Veins act as a blood reservoir"],["","What controls the rate of blood flow to a tissue?","What controls the rate of blood flow to a tissue?","Tissue need; An active tissue (e.g. working muscle) gets more blood"],["","How does the muscular (venous) pump work?","How does the muscular (venous) pump work?","Contracting muscles compress veins within a closed compartment (bounded by deep fascia); This pushes blood upwards towards the heart; Valves stop it falling back"]]},{"id":9089,"t":"Capillaries","s":"A biopsy of the liver shows capillaries with large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane. Which function might this support?","o":["Removing defective red cells and bacteria","Forming a barrier that keeps toxins out of tissue","Rapid filtration to make urine","Delivering hormones into the blood","Exchanging CO₂ for O₂"],"e":"The liver has <b>sinusoidal</b> capillaries (large gaps, incomplete BM, letting certain substances and even cells through); liver capillaries <b>remove defective red blood cells and bacteria</b>.","w":["That is the brain.","Kidney.","Endocrine.","Lungs."],"k":"Liver sinusoids: remove defective RBCs and bacteria.","rn":[["","How do capillaries support the liver?","How do capillaries support the liver?","Remove defective red blood cells and bacteria"]]},{"id":9090,"t":"Capillaries","s":"Which statement about the brain's capillaries is correct?","o":["Continuous capillaries forming the BBB","Fenestrated capillaries forming the BBB","Sinusoidal capillaries forming the BBB","Continuous capillaries carrying chyle","Fenestrated capillaries for rapid exchange"],"e":"The nervous system has <b>continuous</b> capillaries (complete lining, only small molecules pass), forming the <b>blood–brain barrier</b> – delivering nutrients and keeping toxins out.","w":["Continuous.","Continuous.","Lacteals.","Continuous."],"k":"Brain: continuous capillaries, BBB.","rn":[["","How do capillaries support the brain?","How do capillaries support the brain?","Form the blood-brain barrier; Deliver nutrients but keep toxins out"]]},{"id":9091,"t":"Capillaries","s":"In the kidneys, what do the peritubular capillaries do?","o":["Filter blood, make urine, absorb water and sodium","Filter blood, help make urine, absorb fats","Let new blood cells into the blood","Remove defective red blood cells and bacteria","Deliver hormones into the blood"],"e":"<b>Kidneys</b>: <b>peritubular capillaries</b> filter blood, help produce urine and absorb water and sodium.","w":["Water and sodium.","Marrow.","That is the liver.","Endocrine."],"k":"Peritubular capillaries.","rn":[["","How do capillaries support the kidneys?","How do capillaries support the kidneys?","Peritubular capillaries can:"]]},{"id":9092,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"A patient has ankle swelling because lymph cannot drain. Which balance of capillary fluid exchange explains why lymphatics normally prevent this?","o":["More filters out than is reabsorbed; lymphatics remove the excess","More is reabsorbed than filters out; lymphatics add the shortfall","Filtration and reabsorption are equal; lymphatics carry proteins only","More filters out than is reabsorbed; veins remove the excess","No fluid leaves the capillaries normally"],"e":"Filtration (~20 L/day) exceeds reabsorption (~17 L/day); lymphatics collect the ~3 L/day excess. If they fail, fluid accumulates – <b>lymphoedema</b>.","w":["Reversed.","Not equal.","Lymphatics.","Fluid leaves."],"k":"Lymphatics clear filtration excess.","rn":[["","What are the functions of the lymphatic system?","What are the functions of the lymphatic system?","Complementary to the blood vascular system; A transport network of delicate tubes throughout the body; Conveys lymph through a network of lymphatic channels; Filters lymph through lymph nodes; Helps manage fluid levels by returning fluid to the bloodstream (venous system); Part of the immune system; Transports specific fats absorbed by the gut"],["","Where does lymph come from?","Where does lymph come from?","Fluid filters out of the blood capillary into the tissues (interstitial fluid); Most goes back into the blood; The leftover is picked up by the blind-ended lymphatic capillary and becomes lymph"],["","What are the 5 jobs of the spleen?","What are the 5 jobs of the spleen?","Filter blood; Store lymphocytes; React to blood borne antigens; Remove old erythrocytes; Recycle iron from haemoglobin"]]},{"id":9093,"t":"Veins","s":"Which vessel would you expect to have the thickest tunica media relative to its size?","o":["A muscular artery","A large elastic (conducting) artery","A medium vein","A venule","A capillary"],"e":"<b>Muscular arteries</b> have the most smooth muscle in the tunica media relative to elastic tissue; veins, venules and lymphatics have thin walls; capillaries have no media.","w":["More elastic tissue than muscle.","Thin.","Thin.","No media."],"k":"Muscular arteries: thick media.","rn":[["","How do you tell an artery from a vein: wall?","How do you tell an artery from a vein: wall?","Artery: thick, muscular media; Vein: thin"]]},{"id":9094,"t":"Veins","s":"Which statement about valves across vessels is correct?","o":["Veins (not head, neck, IVC) and larger lymphatics have valves","Arteries and veins have valves; lymphatics do not","Veins (including head, neck, IVC) have valves; lymphatics do not","All vessels including capillaries have valves","Only the aorta and IVC have valves"],"e":"<b>Veins</b> have valves (absent in IVC, head and neck) and <b>larger lymphatic vessels</b> have valves; <b>arteries have none</b>.","w":["Arteries none.","Absent there.","Capillaries none.","Not stated."],"k":"Valves in veins and lymphatics.","rn":[["","Where are unidirectional valves found (and not found) in veins?","Where are unidirectional valves found (and not found) in veins?","Present in most veins throughout the body; Absent in veins of the head and neck and IVC"],["","What are the features of lymphatic vessels?","What are the features of lymphatic vessels?","Porous, blind-ended capillaries; They converge into larger vessels; Drain into the large veins at the root of the neck, at the venous angle; Have small valves to prevent backflow; Thinner than veins"]]},{"id":9095,"t":"Arteries","s":"An atheromatous plaque blocks vessel <b>4</b>. Which vessel is it?","e":"4 is the left common carotid artery, the second branch of the arch, between the brachiocephalic trunk (1) and left subclavian (5).","k":"4 = left common carotid.","img":{"src":"9/aorticarch","cap":"Aorta and its branches, anterior view. Labels removed; vessels numbered."},"a":["left common carotid artery","left common carotid","common carotid","left carotid","left common carotid a"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 branches of the arch of the aorta?","What are the 3 branches of the arch of the aorta?","Brachiocephalic trunk; Left common carotid artery; Left subclavian artery"]]},{"id":9096,"t":"Arteries","s":"Which statement about the external iliac and common iliac arteries is correct?","o":["Common iliacs split into external (lower limbs) and internal iliacs","Common iliacs split into external (pelvis) and internal iliacs","Common iliacs split into internal (lower limbs) and external iliacs","Common iliacs arise from the arch and supply the lower limbs","External iliacs split into common and internal iliacs"],"e":"The abdominal aorta ends by splitting into the <b>common iliacs</b>, which divide into <b>external</b> (lower limbs) and <b>internal</b> (pelvic organs) iliacs.","w":["Lower limbs.","Pelvis is internal.","Abdominal aorta.","Wrong order."],"k":"Iliac branching.","rn":[["","What do the common iliac arteries split into?","What do the common iliac arteries split into?","External iliac arteries; Internal iliac arteries"]]},{"id":9097,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"What is the significance of very little pressure remaining after the capillaries?","o":["Venous flow needs muscle, breathing and venous smooth muscle","Venous flow is pushed mainly by the heart’s contraction","Venous flow depends mainly on arterial pressure","Venous flow needs lymph to push it","Venous flow stops when standing"],"e":"Little pressure remains after capillaries, so <b>venous return is not pushed by the heart</b>; it depends on <b>skeletal muscle</b>, <b>respiratory movements</b> and <b>venous smooth muscle</b>.","w":["Little pressure remains.","Little remains.","Not stated.","Pooling, not stopping."],"k":"Low post-capillary pressure → need venous return mechanisms.","rn":[["","What drives venous return?","What drives venous return?","Skeletal muscle action; Respiratory movements; Constriction of smooth muscle in the venous walls"],["","What drives lymph flow?","What drives lymph flow?","A pressure gradient created by:; Vascular smooth muscle; Skeletal muscle contraction; Respiratory movements"],["","What are the 7 principles of circulation?","What are the 7 principles of circulation?","Blood flows down a pressure gradient (from high to low pressure); Flow matches tissue need; Arterial pressure is controlled separately from local blood flow and cardiac output; Velocity varies inversely with cross-sectional area; Resistance opposes flow (mostly due to vessel diameter); Little pressure is left after the capillaries; Venous return is not pushed by the heart; Physical laws apply (Ohm's law, Poiseuille's law)"]]},{"id":9098,"t":"Capillaries","s":"Which tissue would most likely contain fenestrated capillaries?","o":["An endocrine gland releasing hormones","Skeletal muscle during exercise","Adipose tissue storing fat","The brain behind its barrier","The spleen filtering blood"],"e":"<b>Fenestrated</b> capillaries are in <b>kidneys, small intestine and endocrine glands</b> (rapid exchange; hormones delivered). Muscle, fat and brain have continuous; spleen has sinusoidal.","w":["Continuous.","Continuous.","Continuous.","Sinusoidal."],"k":"Endocrine glands: fenestrated.","rn":[["","Fenestrated capillaries","Fenestrated capillaries","Small openings (fenestrae) for rapid exchange; Found in the kidneys, small intestine and endocrine glands"]]},{"id":9099,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"Which of the following is the best description of the venous angle?","o":["Junction of internal jugular and subclavian veins, where lymph enters","Junction of external and internal iliac veins, where lymph enters","The start of the thoracic duct in the abdomen","A valve at the entry of the IVC into the heart","The point where the aorta arches"],"e":"Lymphatics drain into the <b>large veins at the root of the neck</b> at the <b>venous angle</b>, where the <b>internal jugular and subclavian</b> veins meet.","w":["Neck.","Cisterna chyli.","Not stated.","Arterial."],"k":"Venous angle = IJV/subclavian junction.","rn":[["","What is the route of lymph from the cisterna chyli?","What is the route of lymph from the cisterna chyli?","Cisterna chyli; Then the thoracic duct; Then the left subclavian vein (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins)"],["","Where does the right lymphatic duct empty?","Where does the right lymphatic duct empty?","At the junction of the right internal jugular and right subclavian veins"]]},{"id":9100,"t":"Lymphatic Organs","s":"Which statement about the spleen is <b>false</b>?","o":["It is a primary lymphoid organ","It is the largest lymphatic organ","It lies in the left upper abdomen","It removes old red cells","It recycles iron from haemoglobin"],"e":"The spleen is <b>secondary</b> lymphoid tissue (where immune cells function). Primary organs producing lymphocytes are the <b>thymus and bone marrow</b>.","w":["True.","True.","True.","True."],"k":"Spleen = secondary lymphoid tissue.","rn":[["","What are the primary lymphoid organs?","What are the primary lymphoid organs?","Thymus; Bone marrow"]]},{"id":9101,"t":"Lymph Nodes and Lymphatic Drainage","s":"A patient's lower limb lymph cannot pass the inguinal region. Which nodes are likely involved?","o":["Superficial inguinal nodes","Popliteal nodes","Axillary nodes","Para-aortic nodes","Supratrochlear nodes"],"e":"Superficial lymphatics of the lower limb follow the <b>great saphenous vein</b> and drain upwards into the <b>superficial inguinal nodes</b>.","w":["Behind the knee, not the inguinal region.","Upper limb.","Deep, later.","Upper limb."],"k":"Leg lymph → inguinal nodes.","rn":[["","Superficial lymph nodes: where are they and where do they drain?","Superficial lymph nodes: where are they and where do they drain?","Superficial to deep fascia; With superficial veins; Drain to deep nodes"]]},{"id":9102,"t":"Arteries","s":"Name the part of the aorta labelled <b>6</b>, from which the great vessels to the head, neck and upper limbs arise.","e":"6 is the arch of the aorta, between the ascending (2) and descending (7) aorta.","k":"6 = arch of aorta.","img":{"src":"9/aorticarch","cap":"Aorta and its branches, anterior view. Labels removed; vessels numbered."},"a":["arch of aorta","aortic arch","arch of the aorta","arch"],"rn":[["","What are the parts of the aorta, in order?","What are the parts of the aorta, in order?","Ascending aorta; Arch of aorta; Thoracic (descending) aorta; Abdominal aorta; Then it splits into the common iliac arteries"]]},{"id":9103,"t":"Veins","s":"Which change would most likely lead to varicose veins?","o":["Raised venous pressure so valves fail","Raised arterial pressure stiffening the walls of veins","Fenestration of the capillaries in the leg","Blockage of lacteals in the gut","Thymic involution after puberty"],"e":"Raised pressure dilates veins; valves can no longer close (<b>incompetent</b>), causing <b>varicose veins</b>.","w":["Venous pressure.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Dilated veins → incompetent valves.","rn":[["","What causes varicose veins?","What causes varicose veins?","Veins dilate because of raised pressure; The valves may not close (incompetent valves); Resulting in varicose veins"]]},{"id":9104,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"Which statement best explains why the lymphatic system is described as “complementary” to the blood vascular system?","o":["It returns excess fluid and proteins not reabsorbed","It returns excess red cells that the capillaries cannot carry back","It pumps fluid alongside the heart","It carries oxygen to tissues the arteries miss","It filters all blood before it reaches the heart"],"e":"The lymphatic system <b>complements</b> the cardiovascular system: it collects the excess filtered fluid (~3 L/day), with proteins and cells, and returns it to the veins.","w":["Not red cells.","No pump.","Not oxygen.","Not stated."],"k":"Lymphatics complement circulation.","rn":[["","What are the functions of the lymphatic system?","What are the functions of the lymphatic system?","Complementary to the blood vascular system; A transport network of delicate tubes throughout the body; Conveys lymph through a network of lymphatic channels; Filters lymph through lymph nodes; Helps manage fluid levels by returning fluid to the bloodstream (venous system); Part of the immune system; Transports specific fats absorbed by the gut"],["","What are the features of lymph (lymphatic fluid)?","What are the features of lymph (lymphatic fluid)?","Originates in tissue capillary beds; Almost identical to interstitial fluid; Contains lymphocytes; Relatively high protein concentration"]]},{"id":9105,"t":"Arteries","s":"Name the vessel labelled <b>1</b>, the first branch of the arch of the aorta.","e":"1 is the brachiocephalic trunk, the first branch of the arch; it supplies the right side of the head, neck and upper limb.","k":"1 = brachiocephalic trunk.","img":{"src":"9/aorticarch","cap":"Aorta and its branches, anterior view. Labels removed; vessels numbered."},"a":["brachiocephalic trunk","brachiocephalic artery","brachiocephalic","innominate artery"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 branches of the arch of the aorta?","What are the 3 branches of the arch of the aorta?","Brachiocephalic trunk; Left common carotid artery; Left subclavian artery"]]},{"id":9106,"t":"Arteries","s":"Which pairing of anastomosis and location is <b>incorrect</b>?","o":["Marginal artery – around the knee","Circle of Willis – brain","Palmar arches – hand","Genicular anastomosis – knee","Anastomoses around the elbow – joint"],"e":"The <b>marginal artery</b> is in the <b>colon</b> (gut). The knee has the <b>genicular</b> anastomosis.","w":["Correct.","Correct.","Correct.","Correct."],"k":"Marginal artery = colon.","rn":[["","Give 4 examples of anastomoses","Give 4 examples of anastomoses","Circle of Willis; Joints: shoulder, elbow, knee; Marginal artery of colon; Palmar arches of hand"]]},{"id":9107,"t":"Capillaries","s":"Which best explains why capillaries are only one cell thick?","o":["Their job is exchange, which a thin wall allows","Their job is storage, which a thin wall allows","They must withstand the highest pressure","They contain valves to stop backflow","They produce lymphocytes"],"e":"Capillaries' main job is <b>exchange</b>; being only endothelium (one cell thick) allows gases, nutrients and waste to pass.","w":["Veins store.","Arteries.","No valves.","Not stated."],"k":"Thin wall for exchange.","rn":[["","What is the main job of capillaries?","What is the main job of capillaries?","Exchange of gases, nutrients and waste between the bloodstream and the tissues (mnemonic: Got No Waste); They carry blood, nutrients and oxygen to cells (mnemonic: BON)"],["","How do you tell an artery from a vein: wall?","How do you tell an artery from a vein: wall?","Artery: thick, muscular media; Vein: thin"]]},{"id":9108,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"Which pairing of vessel and blood-volume percentage is correct?","o":["Pulmonary vessels – 9–10%","Heart – 64–70%","Systemic arteries – 5–7%","Systemic capillaries – 10–15%","Systemic veins – 7%"],"e":"Systemic veins 64–70%; arteries 10–15%; capillaries 5–7%; <b>pulmonary vessels 9–10%</b>; heart 7%.","w":["7%.","10–15%.","5–7%.","64–70%."],"k":"Blood distribution percentages.","rn":[["","Where is the rest of the blood, that isn't in the vessels?","Where is the rest of the blood, that isn't in the vessels?","Heart: 7%; Pulmonary vessels: 9-10%"]]},{"id":9109,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"Name the saccular dilation in the abdomen that forms the lowest part of the thoracic duct.","e":"The cisterna chyli collects lymph from the lower limbs and abdomen and continues as the thoracic duct.","k":"Cisterna chyli.","a":["cisterna chyli","cysterna chyli","cisterna"],"rn":[["","Cisterna chyli","Cisterna chyli","A saccular dilation in the abdomen; The most inferior part of the thoracic duct"]]},{"id":9110,"t":"Clinical: The Lymphatic System in Disease","s":"Lymphadenopathy can be caused by a threadworm infection. Name it.","e":"Filariasis (threadworm infection) is a cause of lymphatic disease/lymphadenopathy.","k":"Filariasis.","a":["filariasis","filarial","lymphatic filariasis"],"rn":[["","What are the causes of lymphadenopathy?","What are the causes of lymphadenopathy?","Infection: bacterial, viral, fungal, or filariasis (threadworm); Skin wounds, e.g. cat scratch (Bartonella henselae); Metastases; Primary malignancy (lymphoma)"]]},{"id":9111,"t":"Capillaries","s":"Which pairing of capillary location and type is correct?","o":["Small intestine – fenestrated","Liver – continuous capillaries","Brain – sinusoidal","Muscle – fenestrated","Spleen – continuous"],"e":"Fenestrated: kidneys, <b>small intestine</b>, endocrine glands. Continuous: nervous system, fat, muscle. Sinusoidal: liver, spleen, bone marrow.","w":["Sinusoidal.","Continuous.","Continuous.","Sinusoidal."],"k":"Capillary type by organ.","rn":[["","Fenestrated capillaries","Fenestrated capillaries","Small openings (fenestrae) for rapid exchange; Found in the kidneys, small intestine and endocrine glands"]]},{"id":9112,"t":"Veins","s":"Which statement about the deep fascia and the muscle pump is correct?","o":["It limits expansion, so contraction compresses the vein","It contracts itself to compress the vein","It contains the valves that stop backflow","It allows the vein to expand outward freely as the muscle contracts","It produces lymph that pushes blood up"],"e":"Contracting muscles compress veins <b>within a closed compartment bounded by deep fascia</b>; the fascia limits outward expansion, so contraction becomes compression, pushing blood upwards.","w":["Muscles contract.","Valves are intimal.","It limits expansion.","Not stated."],"k":"Deep fascia enables the pump."},{"id":9113,"t":"Arteries","s":"Name the vessel labelled <b>3</b>, arising from the root of the aorta to supply the heart.","e":"The coronary arteries arise from the ascending aorta/aortic root; 3 is the right coronary artery. Coronary arteries are functional end arteries, so blockage causes a heart attack.","k":"3 = right coronary artery.","img":{"src":"9/aorticarch","cap":"Aorta and its branches, anterior view. Labels removed; vessels numbered."},"a":["right coronary artery","right coronary","rca"],"rn":[["","What does the brachiocephalic artery split into?","What does the brachiocephalic artery split into?","Right common carotid artery; Right subclavian artery"],["","What are the examples of end arteries?","What are the examples of end arteries?","Splenic; Renal; Coronary; Central artery of the retina; Vasa recta of the mesenteric arteries; Appendicular artery (from its midpoint distally); Perforating arteries"]]},{"id":9114,"t":"Lymph Nodes and Lymphatic Drainage","s":"Lymph from the abdominal viscera and lower limbs most likely travels through which nodes before the cisterna chyli?","o":["Deep nodes named after vessels, e.g. para-aortic and iliac","Superficial nodes named by number, e.g. group 1 and 2","Deep nodes in the neck, e.g. cervical and supraclavicular","Upper limb nodes, e.g. axillary and supratrochlear","Thymic and splenic nodes in the thorax"],"e":"Deep nodes lie near the vasculature and viscera and are <b>named after neighbouring structures</b>: <b>pre-aortic, para-aortic, common iliac, internal and external iliac</b>.","w":["Named after structures.","Neck.","Upper limb.","Not stated."],"k":"Abdominal deep nodes.","rn":[["","Give 5 groups of deep lymph nodes.","Give 5 groups of deep lymph nodes.","Pre-aortic; Para-aortic; Common iliac; Internal iliac; External iliac"],["","How are deep lymph nodes named?","How are deep lymph nodes named?","After neighbouring structures (near vessels and viscera)"]]},{"id":9115,"t":"Capillaries","s":"Which statement about capillary structure is correct?","o":["Slides mention an outer layer, but the core fact is endothelium on a BM","Slides mention three tunics, but the core fact is a muscular media","Slides mention valves, but the core fact is endothelium on a BM","Slides mention 8–10 mm, but the core fact is 8–10 µm","Slides mention fenestrae, but the core fact is all are continuous"],"e":"Earlier slides say capillaries have <b>only endothelium</b> (on a basement membrane); a later slide describes an outer protective layer around endothelial cells. The core, consistent fact: capillaries are endothelium on a basement membrane, with endothelial cells controlling flow of fluid, nutrients and gases.","w":["No tunics.","No valves.","Not the confusion.","Types differ."],"k":"Capillaries: endothelium + BM.","rn":[["","What are the 2 layers of cells in a capillary?","What are the 2 layers of cells in a capillary?","Endothelial cells (inner layer); Epithelial cells (protective outer layer)"],["","Tunica Intima in Arteries","Tunica Intima in Arteries","elastic membrane lining; smooth surface; endothelium layer"]]},{"id":9116,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"Which statement about arterial and venous pressure is correct?","o":["Arterial high, venous low; explains wall thickness","Arterial low, venous high; explains wall thickness","Arterial and venous pressures are equal","Capillary pressure is highest of all","Lymphatic pressure is highest of all"],"e":"Arteries carry blood at <b>high pressure</b> (thick walls); veins at <b>low pressure</b> (thin walls, need valves and the muscle pump).","w":["Reversed.","Not equal.","Arterial.","Not stated."],"k":"High arterial, low venous pressure.","rn":[["","What are the 7 principles of circulation?","What are the 7 principles of circulation?","Blood flows down a pressure gradient (from high to low pressure); Flow matches tissue need; Arterial pressure is controlled separately from local blood flow and cardiac output; Velocity varies inversely with cross-sectional area; Resistance opposes flow (mostly due to vessel diameter); Little pressure is left after the capillaries; Venous return is not pushed by the heart; Physical laws apply (Ohm's law, Poiseuille's law)"],["","How do you tell an artery from a vein: pressure?","How do you tell an artery from a vein: pressure?","Artery: high; Vein: low"]]},{"id":9117,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"Which statement about lymph composition is correct?","o":["Mainly interstitial fluid with lymphocytes and protein","Mainly plasma with red cells, debris and protein","Mainly interstitial fluid with no cells and very little protein","Mainly chyle with lymphocytes and fat","Mainly water identical to urine"],"e":"Lymph is mainly <b>interstitial fluid</b> with variable amounts of <b>lymphocytes, bacteria, cellular debris, plasma proteins</b> and other cells; relatively high protein.","w":["Interstitial fluid.","Contains cells and relatively high protein.","Chyle is GI lymph.","Not stated."],"k":"Lymph composition.","rn":[["","What are the features of lymph (lymphatic fluid)?","What are the features of lymph (lymphatic fluid)?","Originates in tissue capillary beds; Almost identical to interstitial fluid; Contains lymphocytes; Relatively high protein concentration"]]},{"id":9118,"t":"Arteries","s":"Name the vessel labelled <b>5</b>, the last branch of the arch, supplying the left upper limb.","e":"The third branch of the arch is the left subclavian artery (5).","k":"5 = left subclavian artery.","img":{"src":"9/aorticarch","cap":"Aorta and its branches, anterior view. Labels removed; vessels numbered."},"a":["left subclavian artery","left subclavian","subclavian","subclavian artery"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 branches of the arch of the aorta?","What are the 3 branches of the arch of the aorta?","Brachiocephalic trunk; Left common carotid artery; Left subclavian artery"]]},{"id":9119,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"Which pathway best describes a cancer spreading from the left lower limb to the blood via lymphatics?","o":["Superficial → inguinal → iliac/para-aortic → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct → left angle","Superficial → inguinal → iliac/para-aortic → cisterna chyli → right duct → right angle","Superficial → popliteal → axillary → thoracic duct → left angle","Deep → inguinal → superficial → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct → left angle","Superficial → inguinal → iliac/para-aortic → thoracic duct → cisterna chyli → left angle"],"e":"Lower-limb lymph: superficial vessels → <b>inguinal nodes</b> → deep (iliac, para-aortic) nodes → <b>cisterna chyli</b> → <b>thoracic duct</b> → <b>left venous angle</b>.","w":["Thoracic duct, left.","Not axillary.","Superficial first.","Cisterna first."],"k":"Lower-limb lymphatic route.","rn":[["","What is the route of lymph from the cisterna chyli?","What is the route of lymph from the cisterna chyli?","Cisterna chyli; Then the thoracic duct; Then the left subclavian vein (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins)"]]},{"id":9120,"t":"Arteries","s":"Which statement best summarises why end arteries matter clinically compared with anastomoses?","o":["End artery block kills tissue; an anastomosis gives another route","End artery block is compensated; an anastomosis kills tissue","Both behave the same, as collateral flow is always present","End arteries have valves that prevent backflow","Anastomoses exist only in the retina and heart"],"e":"In an <b>anastomosis</b>, blocked tissue is <b>still supplied</b> by other branches; with an <b>end artery</b>, you get <b>tissue death</b> beyond the block (e.g. retina, heart).","w":["Reversed.","Not the same.","No valves.","Retina/heart are end arteries."],"k":"End artery vs anastomosis.","rn":[["","Anastomosis","Anastomosis","A network or connection formed by the union of branches (e.g. of blood vessels), giving intercommunication; Ensures continued tissue perfusion when one artery is compromised"],["","Functional end artery","Functional end artery","An end artery where anastomoses exist; but are ineffectual (too small to keep the tissue alive)"],["","Why are end arteries clinically important?","Why are end arteries clinically important?","Occlusion causes serious supply problems; Leading to tissue death and loss of function"]]},{"id":9121,"t":"Arteries","s":"A 65-year-old man suddenly loses vision in one eye. Examination shows that the central artery of his retina is blocked by a small clot. Despite rapid treatment, the part of the retina it supplies is permanently damaged. A student asks why a blocked artery elsewhere, such as around the knee, often causes much less damage.<br><br>Which explanation is correct?","o":["The retinal artery is an end artery; the knee has anastomoses giving collateral flow","The retinal artery is an elastic artery that smooths out pressure","The knee arteries have no anastomoses, so damage is always worse there","The retinal artery has valves that close when it is blocked, stopping any backflow of blood to the tissue","The retina is supplied by sinusoidal capillaries only"],"e":"Step 1: an end (terminal) artery supplies a portion of tissue without contribution from other arteries or significant collateral circulation; the central artery of the retina is one example, so occlusion causes tissue death and loss of function. Step 2: by contrast, joints such as the knee have anastomoses (connections between branches) that keep tissue perfused when one artery is compromised.","w":["Large elastic arteries are those near the heart, such as the aorta.","The knee does have anastomoses.","Arteries do not have valves; veins do.","The retina's problem is its end-artery supply, not its capillary type."],"k":"End arteries lack collaterals → occlusion causes tissue death.","rn":[["","End (terminal) artery","End (terminal) artery","Supplies a portion of tissue without contribution from other arteries or any significant collateral circulation; Does not anastomose with its neighbours"]]},{"id":9122,"t":"Veins","s":"A 50-year-old hairdresser who stands all day develops twisted, swollen veins in her legs. Her doctor explains that blood returning from her legs normally travels mostly against gravity. Raised pressure has dilated her veins, so a structure inside them no longer works properly.<br><br>Which structure has failed, and what is the normal mechanism of venous return in the legs?","o":["Venous valves; the muscle pump compressing veins inside deep fascia","Venous valves; ventricular contraction pushing blood back up through the leg veins to the heart","The internal elastic lamina; arterial pressure pushes blood back","Lymphatic valves; the thoracic duct pumps blood upwards","Capillary fenestrae; filtration returns blood"],"e":"Step 1: venous valves (inward projections of the intima) make blood flow towards the heart; if veins dilate from raised pressure, the valves may not close (incompetent valves), resulting in varicose veins. Step 2: venous return is not the direct result of ventricular contraction; in the legs it relies on the muscular pump, where contracting muscles compress veins within a closed compartment bounded by deep fascia, with valves preventing backflow.","w":["Venous flow is not the direct result of ventricular contraction.","Very little pressure is left after the capillaries to drive venous return.","Lymphatic vessels carry lymph, not blood, and have no pump.","Capillary fenestrae allow exchange, not venous return."],"k":"Incompetent valves → varicose veins; legs rely on the muscle pump.","rn":[["","How does the muscular (venous) pump work?","How does the muscular (venous) pump work?","Contracting muscles compress veins within a closed compartment (bounded by deep fascia); This pushes blood upwards towards the heart; Valves stop it falling back"]]},{"id":9123,"t":"Veins","s":"A histology student examines a slide with two vessels next to each other. Vessel A has a thick muscular media, a small round lumen and a prominent internal elastic lamina. Vessel B has a thin wall, a large collapsed lumen, little elastic tissue and a valve cusp. The student must say which carries blood at higher pressure and which acts as a reservoir.<br><br>Which statement is correct?","o":["A is an artery at high pressure; B is a vein that acts as a reservoir","A is a vein at high pressure; B is an artery reservoir","A is a lymphatic vessel; B is a capillary","Both are arteries; B is a pulmonary artery carrying deoxygenated blood","A is a vein, because valves are found in the tunica media of veins with thick muscular walls"],"e":"Step 1: a thick muscular media, small round lumen and prominent internal elastic lamina identify an artery, which carries blood at high pressure. Step 2: a thin wall, large often collapsed lumen, little elastic tissue and valves identify a vein; veins hold most of the blood (64–70% in systemic veins) and act as a reservoir.","w":["The features are reversed.","Capillaries have only endothelium, and lymphatics are thinner than veins.","Arteries do not have valves.","Valves are inward projections of the intima of veins, and veins have thin walls."],"k":"Artery: thick media, round lumen, IEL; vein: thin, large lumen, valves.","rn":[["","Image card: Which is the artery and which is the vein?","Which is the artery and which is the vein?","1 Artery: thick, round, muscular wall and small lumen; 2 Vein: thin wall, wide, flattened / collapsed lumen; Arteries are thicker because they carry blood at higher pressure"]]},{"id":9124,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"A physiology student measures blood flow velocity at different points in the circulation. Velocity is high in the aorta, falls to its lowest value in the capillaries, and rises again in the veins. The total cross-sectional area of the capillaries is much larger than that of the aorta.<br><br>Which principle explains this pattern, and why is it useful?","o":["Velocity varies inversely with cross-sectional area; slow capillary flow allows exchange","Velocity varies directly with cross-sectional area; fast capillary flow speeds exchange","Velocity depends only on vessel wall thickness; thick walls slow flow","Ventricular contraction pushes blood fastest through the capillaries, so that more oxygen is delivered to tissues","Resistance is lowest in capillaries, so velocity is lowest there"],"e":"Step 1: the velocity of blood flow varies inversely with the total cross-sectional area of the vessels: as total area increases, velocity decreases. Step 2: the capillaries have the largest total cross-section, so flow is slowest there, giving time for exchange of gases and nutrients.","w":["The relationship is inverse, and capillary flow is slowest.","Velocity is linked to cross-sectional area, not wall thickness.","Flow is slowest, not fastest, in the capillaries.","The principle is about total cross-sectional area, not low resistance."],"k":"Largest total cross-section in capillaries → slowest flow → exchange.","rn":[["","How does the velocity of blood flow relate to the cross-sectional area of the vessels?","How does the velocity of blood flow relate to the cross-sectional area of the vessels?","Inversely; As the total cross-sectional area increases, velocity decreases"],["","What are the 7 principles of circulation?","What are the 7 principles of circulation?","Blood flows down a pressure gradient (from high to low pressure); Flow matches tissue need; Arterial pressure is controlled separately from local blood flow and cardiac output; Velocity varies inversely with cross-sectional area; Resistance opposes flow (mostly due to vessel diameter); Little pressure is left after the capillaries; Venous return is not pushed by the heart; Physical laws apply (Ohm's law, Poiseuille's law)"]]},{"id":9125,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"A 70-year-old woman has a narrowed artery to her leg, with the inside diameter reduced by fatty deposits. She gets calf pain when she walks because not enough blood reaches the muscle. Her doctor explains the relationship between vessel diameter, resistance and flow, mentioning Poiseuille's law.<br><br>Which explanation is correct?","o":["Smaller diameter raises resistance, so flow falls","Smaller diameter lowers resistance, so flow rises","Diameter does not affect resistance; only viscosity matters","Smaller diameter raises velocity, which increases blood flow to the muscle overall during exercise","Narrowing affects veins only, not arteries"],"e":"Step 1: resistance is a force that opposes the flow of a fluid, and in blood vessels most resistance is due to vessel diameter (Poiseuille's law, radius to the fourth power). Step 2: as diameter decreases, resistance increases and blood flow decreases, so her calf muscle gets too little blood during walking.","w":["Narrowing raises resistance and reduces flow.","The lecture says most resistance is due to diameter.","Narrowing reduces overall flow.","Arterial narrowing reduces arterial flow."],"k":"Diameter ↓ → resistance ↑ → flow ↓.","rn":[["","What happens to resistance and blood flow as vessel diameter decreases?","What happens to resistance and blood flow as vessel diameter decreases?","Resistance increases; Blood flow decreases"]]},{"id":9126,"t":"Capillaries","s":"A pathologist looks at capillaries from three organs. In organ X, the capillaries have small openings allowing rapid exchange. In organ Y, the lining is complete and only small molecules pass, forming a barrier that keeps toxins out. In organ Z, there are gaps large enough to let cells through and an incomplete basement membrane, and the organ removes defective red blood cells.<br><br>Which organs are most likely X, Y and Z?","o":["X: kidney; Y: brain; Z: liver","X: brain; Y: kidney; Z: muscle","X: liver; Y: small intestine; Z: brain","X: muscle; Y: endocrine gland; Z: kidney","X: brain; Y: liver; Z: kidney, because it filters blood through large gaps"],"e":"Step 1: fenestrated capillaries (small openings, rapid exchange) are found in the kidneys, small intestine and endocrine glands; continuous capillaries (complete lining) in the nervous system, fat and muscle; sinusoidal capillaries (gaps, incomplete basement membrane) in the liver, spleen and bone marrow. Step 2: brain capillaries form the blood–brain barrier, and liver capillaries remove defective red blood cells, giving X = kidney, Y = brain, Z = liver.","w":["Brain capillaries are continuous, not fenestrated.","Liver capillaries are sinusoidal, and brain capillaries are continuous.","Muscle capillaries are continuous.","Kidney capillaries are fenestrated, not sinusoidal."],"k":"Fenestrated: kidney/gut/endocrine; continuous: brain/muscle; sinusoidal: liver/spleen."},{"id":9127,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"A 55-year-old man has a tumour blocking his thoracic duct. He develops swelling of both legs and his abdomen. However, his right arm and the right side of his head and chest are not swollen. Lymph from his abdominal organs normally collects in a sac before entering the duct.<br><br>Which statement best explains his pattern of swelling?","o":["The thoracic duct drains about three-quarters of the body; the right lymphatic duct drains the right arm, head, neck and chest","The right lymphatic duct drains both legs, so they swell","The thoracic duct drains only the right arm","The cisterna chyli drains into the right subclavian vein","Lymph returns to the heart by its own pump, which has failed in his legs"],"e":"Step 1: the cisterna chyli in the abdomen receives lymph from the abdominal viscera, walls, pelvis, perineum and lower limbs and drains through the thoracic duct into the left venous angle. Step 2: the thoracic duct drains roughly three-quarters of the body, while the right lymphatic duct drains the right arm and right half of the head, neck and chest, so these areas are spared.","w":["The right lymphatic duct does not drain the legs.","The thoracic duct drains much more than the right arm.","The thoracic duct joins the left subclavian vein region.","The lymphatic system has no pump."],"k":"Thoracic duct: legs, abdomen, left upper body; right duct: right upper quarter.","rn":[["","What does the right lymphatic duct drain?","What does the right lymphatic duct drain?","Most of the right upper quadrant of the body; Right upper trunk; Right upper limb; Right side of the head and neck"]]},{"id":9128,"t":"Clinical: The Lymphatic System in Disease","s":"A 48-year-old woman has breast cancer surgery during which several lymph nodes are removed from her armpit, followed by radiation therapy. Months later, her arm on that side becomes persistently swollen. She has no family history of swelling and her lymphatics developed normally in childhood.<br><br>What is the diagnosis, and which type is it?","o":["Lymphoedema; secondary (acquired) from surgery and radiation","Lymphoedema; primary (inherited) from malformed lymphatics","Lymphadenitis; tender nodes from a bacterial infection","Ascending lymphangitis; needing urgent treatment for infection of the lymph vessels","Varicose veins from incompetent valves"],"e":"Step 1: blockage or damage to the lymphatics causes lymphoedema, swelling from trapped lymph. Step 2: primary lymphoedema is inherited, from disrupted development of the lymphatic system, while secondary lymphoedema is acquired, e.g. from deliberate surgical removal of nodes in cancer surgery or radiation therapy, which fits her history.","w":["Her lymphatics developed normally, so it is not primary.","Lymphadenitis is inflammation of nodes, usually bacterial, with tender nodes.","Lymphangitis is an infection of lymph vessels.","Varicose veins are dilated veins, not trapped lymph."],"k":"Node removal / radiation → secondary lymphoedema.","rn":[["","What are the causes of secondary lymphoedema?","What are the causes of secondary lymphoedema?","Trauma (including accidental surgical injury); Deliberate surgical removal (e.g. nodes removed in cancer surgery); Infection; Radiation therapy; Obstruction"]]},{"id":9129,"t":"The Lymphatic System","s":"After a meal rich in fat, a researcher samples lymph from two places in an animal. Lymph from the leg is clear and almost identical to interstitial fluid, with lymphocytes and a fairly high protein content. Lymph from vessels in the wall of the small intestine is opaque and milky.<br><br>What is the milky fluid, and why does it look milky?","o":["Chyle in lacteals, containing absorbed fats packaged as chylomicrons","Blood from fenestrated capillaries, containing red cells","Lymph full of bacteria trapped by lymph nodes","Interstitial fluid with extra lymphocytes released from the bone marrow and the thymus gland","Bile from the liver's sinusoidal capillaries"],"e":"Step 1: chyle is a special type of lymph from the gastrointestinal tract, carried in lymph capillaries called lacteals. Step 2: in the small intestine, long-chain fatty acids are coated with protein and packaged with cholesterol to form chylomicrons, giving chyle its opaque, milk-like appearance.","w":["Lacteals carry lymph, not blood.","The milky look is from fats, not trapped bacteria.","Lymphocytes do not make lymph milky.","The lymphatic fluid in lacteals is chyle, not bile."],"k":"Lacteals carry chyle: milky from chylomicrons (fat).","rn":[["","Chyle","Chyle","A special type of lymph from the gastrointestinal tract; Contains fats (cholesterol, glycerol, fatty acids) from intestinal absorption; Milk-like (opaque, milky) appearance; Carried in lacteals"],["","Do absorbed fats enter the blood capillaries or the lacteals?","Do absorbed fats enter the blood capillaries or the lacteals?","The lacteals (as chylomicrons)"]]},{"id":9130,"t":"Lymph Nodes and Lymphatic Drainage","s":"A 12-year-old girl is scratched by a cat on her forearm. Two weeks later, she has swollen, tender lymph nodes in her armpit on the same side. Her doctor explains the role of lymph nodes as lymph drains from the area of the scratch.<br><br>What is the most likely explanation, and what are lymph nodes doing?","o":["Lymphadenopathy from infection; nodes filter lymph and defend against antigens","Lymphoma; a primary cancer of lymphocytes","Metastases; carcinoma spreading from the forearm","Lymphoedema; nodes have been surgically removed","Primary lymphoid organ swelling; the nodes are producing new lymphocytes, as the thymus and bone marrow do"],"e":"Step 1: lymphadenopathy (swollen nodes) can be caused by infection or skin wounds such as a cat scratch (Bartonella henselae), and tender nodes fit lymphadenitis. Step 2: all lymph passes through lymph nodes, which act as filters that trap and phagocytose particulate matter and detect and defend against foreign antigens.","w":["The history of a cat scratch points to infection, not lymphoma.","There is no cancer here; the cause is a skin wound.","Her nodes have not been removed.","Lymph nodes are secondary lymphoid tissue; thymus and bone marrow are primary."],"k":"Cat scratch → lymphadenopathy; nodes filter and defend.","rn":[["","What are the features of lymph nodes?","What are the features of lymph nodes?","Small, bean-shaped tissues along lymphatic vessels; All lymph passes through lymph nodes; Afferent vessels take lymph to the node; efferent vessels take lymph away; Elaborate filters that trap and phagocytose particulate matter; Detect and defend against foreign antigens"]]},{"id":9501,"t":"Arteries","s":"Which labelling of layers A, B and C is correct?","o":["A tunica adventitia (externa); B tunica media; C tunica intima","A tunica intima; B tunica media; C tunica adventitia","A tunica media; B tunica adventitia; C tunica intima","A tunica adventitia; B tunica intima; C tunica media","A tunica media; B tunica intima; C tunica adventitia"],"w":["The intima (C) lines the lumen; the adventitia (A) is outermost.","B, the thick smooth muscle layer, is the media.","The muscular media lies between intima and adventitia.","B is the thickest layer of smooth muscle: the media."],"e":"From outside in: tunica adventitia/externa (A), tunica media (B, smooth muscle), tunica intima (C, endothelium E and internal elastic membrane D).","k":"Outside in: adventitia, media, intima.","img":{"src":"9/o_arterywall","cap":"Structure of an artery wall. Image: BruceBlaus (Blausen Medical), CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the three layers (tunics) of the vessel wall, from outside to inside?","What are the three layers (tunics) of the vessel wall, from outside to inside?","Tunica externa (adventitia); Tunica media; Tunica intima; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":9502,"t":"Arteries","s":"Name the wavy elastic layer labelled <b>D</b>, separating the intima from the media.","a":["internal elastic lamina","internal elastic membrane","internal elastic"],"e":"D is the internal elastic lamina (membrane); F is the external elastic membrane at the outer edge of the media.","k":"D = internal elastic lamina.","img":{"src":"9/o_arterywall","cap":"Structure of an artery wall. Image: BruceBlaus (Blausen Medical), CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What separates the tunica media from the tunica intima?","What separates the tunica media from the tunica intima?","The internal elastic lamina"]]},{"id":9503,"t":"Arteries","s":"Name the single layer of flat cells labelled <b>E</b>, lining the lumen.","a":["endothelium","endothelial cells","endothelial"],"e":"E is the endothelium: flat cells lining all vessels, part of the tunica intima.","k":"E = endothelium.","img":{"src":"9/o_arterywall","cap":"Structure of an artery wall. Image: BruceBlaus (Blausen Medical), CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Endothelium","Endothelium","A single layer of squamous epithelial cells; The internal lining in the tunica intima of blood and lymphatic vessels"],["","Tunica Intima in Arteries","Tunica Intima in Arteries","elastic membrane lining; smooth surface; endothelium layer"]]},{"id":9504,"t":"Veins","s":"Layer <b>B</b> is much thicker in arteries than in veins. Why?","o":["It is the smooth-muscle media, needed because arteries carry blood at higher pressure","It is the adventitia, needed to anchor arteries to bone","It is the intima, needed to stop blood clotting","It is the media, needed because veins carry more oxygen","It is the elastic lamina, needed for valve formation"],"w":["A anchors the vessel; B is the muscular media.","C (intima) is the lining.","Veins carry deoxygenated blood (mostly); pressure is the reason.","B is mainly smooth muscle, not a single lamina."],"e":"The tunica media (smooth muscle and elastic fibres) is thicker in arteries because they carry blood at higher pressure; veins are thin-walled reservoirs.","k":"Thick media = high pressure.","img":{"src":"9/o_arterywall","cap":"Structure of an artery wall. Image: BruceBlaus (Blausen Medical), CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Image card: Which is the artery and which is the vein?","Which is the artery and which is the vein?","1 Artery: thick, round, muscular wall and small lumen; 2 Vein: thin wall, wide, flattened / collapsed lumen; Arteries are thicker because they carry blood at higher pressure"]]},{"id":9505,"t":"Veins","s":"Venous blood from the head and upper limbs returns to the right atrium through vessel <b>A</b>. Name it.","a":["superior vena cava","svc"],"e":"A is the superior vena cava; E is the inferior vena cava.","k":"A = SVC.","img":{"src":"9/o_heart","cap":"Heart and great vessels, anterior view. Image: Wapcaplet, CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; some labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the 2 venae cavae?","What are the 2 venae cavae?","SVC (superior vena cava); IVC (inferior vena cava)"]]},{"id":9506,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"Vessel <b>C</b> leaves the right ventricle and carries deoxygenated blood to the lungs. Name it.","a":["pulmonary artery","pulmonary trunk","pulmonary arteries"],"e":"C is the pulmonary artery: an artery that carries deoxygenated blood (arteries carry blood away from the heart, not always oxygenated).","k":"C = pulmonary artery.","img":{"src":"9/o_heart","cap":"Heart and great vessels, anterior view. Image: Wapcaplet, CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; some labels removed."},"rn":[["","Are arteries and veins defined by oxygen content or by direction of flow?","Are arteries and veins defined by oxygen content or by direction of flow?","Direction of flow; The pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood, but they are still arteries because they carry blood away from the heart; MNEMONIC"],["","Pulmonary Vein","Pulmonary Vein","Vein that carries oxygenated blood; from the lungs; to the left atrium of the heart"]]},{"id":9507,"t":"Intro to the Circulatory System","s":"Which statement about vessels C and D is correct?","o":["C carries deoxygenated blood away from the heart; D carries oxygenated blood to the heart","C carries oxygenated blood away from the heart; D carries deoxygenated blood to the heart","Both carry oxygenated blood, as both are arteries","Both carry deoxygenated blood, as both are veins","C is a vein and D is an artery"],"w":["C is the pulmonary artery (deoxygenated); D the pulmonary vein (oxygenated).","D is a vein: it carries blood to the heart.","C is an artery and D a vein.","Direction defines artery vs vein: C away, D towards the heart."],"e":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins towards it, whatever its oxygen content: the pulmonary artery (C) is deoxygenated, the pulmonary vein (D) oxygenated.","k":"Artery = away from heart.","img":{"src":"9/o_heart","cap":"Heart and great vessels, anterior view. Image: Wapcaplet, CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; some labels removed."},"rn":[["","Are arteries and veins defined by oxygen content or by direction of flow?","Are arteries and veins defined by oxygen content or by direction of flow?","Direction of flow; The pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood, but they are still arteries because they carry blood away from the heart; MNEMONIC"],["","Pulmonary Vein","Pulmonary Vein","Vein that carries oxygenated blood; from the lungs; to the left atrium of the heart"]]},{"id":9508,"t":"Arteries","s":"Name the large elastic artery labelled <b>B</b>, which stretches with each beat and recoils between beats.","a":["aorta","arch of aorta","aortic arch","ascending aorta"],"e":"B is the aorta, a large elastic (conducting) artery.","k":"B = aorta.","img":{"src":"9/o_heart","cap":"Heart and great vessels, anterior view. Image: Wapcaplet, CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; some labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the parts of the aorta, in order?","What are the parts of the aorta, in order?","Ascending aorta; Arch of aorta; Thoracic (descending) aorta; Abdominal aorta; Then it splits into the common iliac arteries"]]}]);
